./iterations/iter_015/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Mohsen Heidari, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Useful terminology for separating fixed-design/path-known regret from sequential or universal regret, matching the run's quantifier issues.","title":"Sequential vs. Fixed Design Regrets in Online Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISIT50566.2022.9834776","venue_year":"IEEE ISIT 2022"},{"authors":"Angelos Assos, Idan Attias, Yuval Dagan, Constantinos Daskalakis, Maxwell K. Fishelson","relevance":"Potential background for recasting the fixed-envelope criterion as an infinite zero-sum game or best-response-oracle feasibility problem.","title":"Online Learning and Solving Infinite Games with an ERM Oracle","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/assos23a.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are characterized by switching or drought-complexity parameters.","title":"Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Vasilis Kontonis, Mingchen Ma, Christos Tzamos","relevance":"Fixed-design online learning with order effects is adjacent to the path-known, horizon-oblivious regime where one sequence must serve many prefixes.","title":"The Gain from Ordering in Online Learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/c9b1fe9c41f1eeec3a659154d575a282-Abstract-Conference.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2023"},{"authors":"Alexander Rakhlin, Karthik Sridharan","relevance":"Predictable-sequence methods are relevant background for paths where delayed fills are predictable or stable-high/stable-low policies nearly match the comparator.","title":"Online Learning with Predictable Sequences","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v30/Rakhlin13.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2013"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The claimed formula may fail in edge cases such as M=1, S=0, h<2 ell, or because the proposed primal mixing ignores an active horizon. Strict crossing also needs eta-loss bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","finite LP regression"],"summary":"Audit the solver's single-drought damping lemma for the two-level formal left-limit path h^M, 0^N, ell, h. Write a complete primal marginal construction and matching dual lambda certificate for D_{M+N+2}=S(1-ell/h), where S=[ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+]_+. Check all boundary cases and then add the strict-crossing implementation.","title":"Audit Single-Drought Damping","why_promising":"This is the newest concrete claimed progress. If correct, it gives an exact local mechanism explaining how old high prices damp later low/high conflicts; if false, finding the failure will immediately refine the two-level program."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Different droughts may interact through shared early quote marginals, and local S_i certificates may neither add nor localize. A path may need nonstable switching policies not captured by stable high or stable low quotes.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dichotomy theorem","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try to decompose arbitrary two-level paths into high-free drought intervals between h occurrences. Define a surplus or queue process related to Q_T=[ell(L_ell(T)-1)-h(L_h(T)-1)]_+. Prove a dichotomy: either Q_T=o(T) and the stable h quote gives o(T) regret, or some comparable-scale prefix has D_n=Omega(n). Treat the case where h eventually stops separately via a stable ell quote.","title":"Two-Level Drought Decomposition","why_promising":"The solver's lemma and stable-high corollary point to a concrete potential function. A successful dichotomy would settle the two-level finite-alphabet localization problem, the current main sandbox."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the fixed-envelope criterion. The hard part is converting repeated augmented certificates into ordinary D_n mass or a real global obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","LP duality","localization"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds or horizon weights beyond a prefix. It should reproduce the boundary defect in the separated-block example and the S term in h^M,0^N,ell,h. Then test whether repeated augmented defects force large ordinary D_n at comparable scales, or instead yield a separator from fixed-envelope feasibility.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Prefix LP","why_promising":"Iteration 14 refuted direct charging of boundary defects to shorter-prefix D_m because late shadow objectives are invisible. Keeping those shadows explicitly is the natural next localization object."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Simple separated blocks are known to be diluted, and fresh quote opportunities may make every proposed separator learnable. Numerical evidence alone would not prove a separator.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite LP experiment","construction"],"summary":"Search for a genuine market path with D_n=o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope by chaining many damped two-level droughts. Tune M_i,N_i so each local conflict is damped relative to total prefix length, while the horizon-optimal quote keeps moving enough to defeat any fixed marginal sequence. Verify candidates against the exact finite-prefix LP, not only stable-quote regret.","title":"Damped-Drought Separator Search","why_promising":"If the gluing implication is false, the solver's damping lemma suggests the separator must exploit many individually small but projectively incompatible shadow conflicts rather than one large block."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The two-level positive-part simplification may not extend cleanly. Nested maxima over comparator levels and learner-side response levels can destroy uncrossing or total-unimodularity arguments.","suggested_lenses":["finite alphabet","proof strategy","recursive potential"],"summary":"Generalize two-level damping to finite alphabets c_1<...<c_k. Use the finite-alphabet dual with next-hit times N_j(t) and last-exceedance times L_j(T) to look for a recursive record-level surplus queue: higher stable comparators damp lower-level conflicts unless a lower-level surplus becomes visible at comparable scale.","title":"Recursive Finite-Alphabet Potential","why_promising":"The two-level case may be too special. A k-level potential would turn the finite-alphabet reduction into structural progress and could expose exactly where nested maxima block localization."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Numerical artifacts can mislead, especially with strict-crossing left limits and near-degenerate dual optima. Small n may miss asymptotic phenomena.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","dual extraction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Build an exact finite-prefix LP experiment harness for finite alphabets in the formal left-limit model. For two-level paths, compare D_n to the stable-high gap Q_T, single-drought S values, and sums of local drought surpluses. Extract dual supports for repeated droughts, alternating paths with h<2 ell, and random interleavings.","title":"Finite-Prefix LP Harness","why_promising":"The current frontier is pattern discovery inside a concrete finite LP. A reliable experiment suite would quickly falsify overstrong decomposition claims and produce dual certificates for explorers to prove."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"External infinite-game and fixed-design results may only repackage compactness or require assumptions absent from market suffix maxima. They should be treated as formulation tools, not imported theorems.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","formal reduction","algorithmic formulation"],"summary":"Recast the fixed-envelope condition as a countably constrained zero-sum game: the learner chooses marginal laws, the adversary chooses horizons or horizon mixtures, and best responses are fixed quotes or finite-prefix dual certificates. Compare this to infinite-game ERM-oracle, fixed-design online learning, and predictable-sequence frameworks to identify assumptions that would imply gluing.","title":"Infinite-Game Fixed-Design Formulation","why_promising":"The web search surfaced adjacent fixed-design and infinite-game sources not yet in the bibliography. This may yield cleaner terminology and possibly algorithmic sufficient conditions between stable-comparator cases and the full compactness criterion."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_015/idea_01/idea.md:6:Audit the solver's single-drought damping lemma for the two-level formal left-limit path h^M, 0^N, ell, h. Write a complete primal marginal construction and matching dual lambda certificate for D_{M+N+2}=S(1-ell/h), where S=[ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+]_+. Check all boundary cases and then add the strict-crossing implementation.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_01/idea.md:9:This is the newest concrete claimed progress. If correct, it gives an exact local mechanism explaining how old high prices damp later low/high conflicts; if false, finding the failure will immediately refine the two-level program.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_01/idea.md:12:The claimed formula may fail in edge cases such as M=1, S=0, h<2 ell, or because the proposed primal mixing ignores an active horizon. Strict crossing also needs eta-loss bookkeeping.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/idea.md:6:Try to decompose arbitrary two-level paths into high-free drought intervals between h occurrences. Define a surplus or queue process related to Q_T=[ell(L_ell(T)-1)-h(L_h(T)-1)]_+. Prove a dichotomy: either Q_T=o(T) and the stable h quote gives o(T) regret, or some comparable-scale prefix has D_n=Omega(n). Treat the case where h eventually stops separately via a stable ell quote.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/idea.md:9:The solver's lemma and stable-high corollary point to a concrete potential function. A successful dichotomy would settle the two-level finite-alphabet localization problem, the current main sandbox.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/idea.md:12:Different droughts may interact through shared early quote marginals, and local S_i certificates may neither add nor localize. A path may need nonstable switching policies not captured by stable high or stable low quotes.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:11:I did not prove this. The best progress is a sharper two-level block calculation showing how old high prices damp later low/high conflicts.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:15:**Single-drought damping lemma.** In the formal left-limit two-level model \(0<\ell<h\), consider
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:17:h^M,\ 0^N,\ \ell,\ h .
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:21:S=\bigl[\ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+\bigr]_+ .
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:25:D_{M+N+2}=S\left(1-\frac{\ell}{h}\right).
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:28:This recovers the usual \(0^N,\ell,h\) conflict when \(M=0\):
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:30:D=N\,\ell\frac{h-\ell}{h}.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:34:\ell(M+N)\le h(M-1),
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:38:Proof sketch: use dual weights \(1-\ell/h\) on the low horizon and \(\ell/h\) on the final high horizon. For the upper bound, quote \(h^-\) outside the high-free drought; on the active drought coordinates mix \(\ell^-\) with probability \(p=S/(hg)\), where \(g=N\) if \(M=0\) and \(g=N+1\) if \(M\ge1\). The low-horizon and final-high deficits both equal \(S(1-\ell/h)\).
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:40:**Stable-high corollary.** For any two-level path, quote \(h^-\) always has regret
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:42:\bigl[\ell(L_\ell(T)-1)-h(L_h(T)-1)\bigr]_+ .
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:48:The damping lemma is local. I do not have a proof that arbitrary two-level paths decompose into such drought certificates without losing projective consistency.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:71:Prove a two-level drought decomposition theorem: either cumulative low-surplus \(S_i\) is linear and yields \(D_n=\Omega(n)\), or a switching/stable quote policy has \(o(T)\) regret.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:75:Extract dual optimizers from small LPs for repeated drought paths and look for a closed-form charging rule.
./iterations/iter_015/solver_attempt.md:81:The damping lemma is proved only for the clean block \(h^M0^N\ell h\), not arbitrary old prefixes.
./iterations/iter_012/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"David Lagziel and Ehud Lehrer","relevance":"Published delayed-information approachability/no-regret source relevant to vector-deficit and simultaneous-horizon formulations.","title":"Approachability with delayed information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2015.01.010","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory 157:425-444, 2015"},{"authors":"Haitian Xie, Ying Zhu, and Denis Shishkin","relevance":"Adjacent price-discrimination/minimax-pricing source; useful context for robust pricing and equal-revenue geometry, not a delayed-fill theorem.","title":"On the limitations of data-based price discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.3982/TE5916","venue_year":"Theoretical Economics 20(1):303-351, 2025"},{"authors":"Ilai Bistritz, Zhengyuan Zhou, Xi Chen, Nicholas Bambos, and Jose Blanchet","relevance":"Delayed adversarial bandit paper with weighted-regret objectives; relevant if the finite-prefix dual is interpreted through horizon-weighted regret.","title":"No Weighted-Regret Learning in Adversarial Bandits with Delays","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v23/20-411.html","venue_year":"Journal of Machine Learning Research 23(139):1-43, 2022"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The naive condition rho_n=o(n) may be too weak: compactness may only control fixed horizons after subsequence extraction. Strict-crossing discontinuities also need a left-limit compactification or eta-implementation.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","topology/compactness","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-prefix LP into an infinite-policy theorem. Define a normalized feasibility quantity such as inf over mu_1,...,mu_{n-1} of max_{T<=n, T>=N} R_T/T, then prove or refute equivalence between this tending to 0 and existence of one horizon-oblivious randomized policy with R_T=o(T) on the deterministic known path.","title":"Compactness For The Prefix LP","why_promising":"This would convert the solver's exact but tautological marginal condition into a precise finite-to-infinite characterization, and it directly targets the main gap in the current solver attempt."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The dual may remain computational rather than structural. Strong duality needs finite support or left-limit action space, and V_T^* must be handled as a supremum under strict crossing.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","proof strategy","certificate search"],"summary":"Derive the exact minimax dual of the finite-prefix problem: min_mu max_{T<=n} [V_T^* - sum_{t<T} g_{mu_t}(M_{t,T})] equals max over horizon weights lambda of sum_T lambda_T V_T^* minus sum_t sup_a sum_{T>t} lambda_T a 1{a<M_{t,T}}. Then interpret dual optimizers as decision-relevant horizon-conflict certificates.","title":"Finite-Prefix Dual Certificates","why_promising":"This generalizes the kappa dual from terminal blocks to arbitrary deterministic paths and may give the right replacement for loose suffix-menu sums."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The comparator sup_a E[...] couples histories globally, so per-history LPs may not compose cleanly. The result may become a stochastic-control tautology unless the dual exposes a simple information modulus.","suggested_lenses":["stochastic extension","conditional duality","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Extend the exact marginal reduction from deterministic paths to exogenous stochastic processes. For policy kernels mu_t(.|history), condition on H_t and write rewards through conditional suffix-max laws P(M_{t,T}>a | H_t). Develop the corresponding conditional LP/dual with probability-weighted hard histories.","title":"Conditional Suffix-Max LP","why_promising":"It keeps the exact-comparator improvement from iteration 12 while moving back toward the original stochastic setting and naturally handles rare-history objections."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"May collapse into a restatement of the full LP. Stable comparators are sufficient but not necessary, and equal-revenue hard blocks are designed to defeat any small stable menu.","suggested_lenses":["examples","sufficient conditions","counterexample search"],"summary":"Generalize the solver's stable-quote corollary. Study conditions under which one quote, a finite quote menu, or slowly varying near-optimal quotes b_T satisfy V_T^* - learner payoff = o(T) for all horizons. Use alternating paths, sparse spikes, and equal-revenue blocks as tests.","title":"Argmax-Stability Conditions","why_promising":"It explains why the alternating path is easy despite linear local suffix-menu cost and may yield readable sufficient conditions between the tautological LP and known loose bounds."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The pseudo-regret comparator may exploit rare branches if their payoff is too large. Horizon accounting must ensure p_i N_i is negligible at all relevant horizons, not only block endpoints.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","probability weighting","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on a low-probability branch, a long equal-revenue block has kappa close to 1/e and long positive payoff delays, but the branch probability p_i makes its expected contribution p_i N_i=o(T_i). Compute beta, gamma, conditional kappa, Delta_T, and regret.","title":"Rare Hard-Block Stress Test","why_promising":"It would sharply demonstrate why essential-sup or unweighted local-hardness conditions are not necessary under expected pseudo-regret."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Ordinary star discrepancy may not control monotone time-varying thresholds with weighted payoffs a_t 1{a_t<c_t}. A failed explicit construction would still leave only the nonconstructive theorem.","suggested_lenses":["constructive proof","low-discrepancy literature","computational experiment"],"summary":"Seek an explicit deterministic sequence replacing the probabilistic-method derandomization of the 1/e upper bound, for example a_t=exp(-u_t) with van der Corput, Kronecker, or other low-discrepancy u_t in [0,1]. Prove a uniform one-sided bound over nonincreasing threshold sequences.","title":"Explicit Equal-Revenue Sequence","why_promising":"It would make the deterministic 1/e known-path upper bound algorithmic and may improve the O(T^{2/3} sqrt log T) error term."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"This may be outside the intended exogenous model, and fully reactive adversaries can make the problem trivially impossible. The explorer should keep this as a boundary audit, not a replacement theorem.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search","sufficiency failure"],"summary":"Probe the endogenous/reactive boundary. Construct examples where future prices depend on outstanding or realized quotes, so M_{t,T} is no longer independent of A_t and the suffix-max marginal formula fails. Identify which sufficiency and upper-bound claims survive only under exogeneity/counterfactual feedback.","title":"Reactive-Price Boundary","why_promising":"It clarifies whether the original delayed market-making model is purely exogenous; many accepted reductions rely on that assumption."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The vector dimension grows with horizon, feedback is horizon-censored, and approachability may reproduce known delayed-learning bounds without a sharper characterization.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","literature audit"],"summary":"Use the finite-prefix dual to define vector deficits indexed by horizons or comparator-threshold pairs, and ask whether Blackwell approachability or delayed approachability yields causal policies for unknown deterministic paths or stochastic kernels. Compare with Lagziel-Lehrer and calibration-decision-loss analogies.","title":"Vector-Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It recasts one quote sequence serving many horizons as a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce algorithms or clean impossibility certificates."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_010/consolidation.md:19:Added verified proof-technology references: Hoeffding 1963, Alon-Spencer 2016, van der Vaart-Wellner 1996, Gao-Wellner 2007, Niederreiter 1992, and Vapnik-Chervonenkis 1971. Key links include:
./iterations/iter_010/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Noga Alon and Joel H. Spencer","relevance":"Standard reference for proving existence of deterministic objects by random construction, concentration, union bounds, and derandomization ideas relevant to the solver's monotone-threshold lemma.","title":"The Probabilistic Method","url":"https://www.wiley.com/en-be/The%2BProbabilistic%2BMethod%2C%2B4th%2BEdition-p-9781119062073","venue_year":"Wiley, 4th ed., 2016"},{"authors":"Aad W. van der Vaart and Jon A. Wellner","relevance":"Primary empirical-process reference for bracketing and uniform laws; useful for replacing the ad hoc grid union bound by a monotone-class entropy proof.","title":"Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes","url":"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2","venue_year":"Springer Series in Statistics, 1996"},{"authors":"Fuchang Gao and Jon A. Wellner","relevance":"Gives metric and bracketing entropy estimates for bounded monotone function classes; relevant if the monotone-threshold derandomization is recast as an empirical-process theorem.","title":"Entropy Estimate For High Dimensional Monotonic Functions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512641","venue_year":"arXiv:math/0512641, 2005"},{"authors":"Harald Niederreiter","relevance":"Classical source on deterministic low-discrepancy sequences and quasi-Monte Carlo, relevant to making the nonconstructive equal-revenue sequence explicit or proving ordinary 1D discrepancy is insufficient.","title":"Random Number Generation and Quasi-Monte Carlo Methods","url":"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/book/10.1137/1.9781611970081","venue_year":"SIAM CBMS-NSF Regional Conference Series, 1992"},{"authors":"L. Kuipers and H. Niederreiter","relevance":"Classical reference for uniform distribution and discrepancy of deterministic sequences, useful for explicit sequence or discrepancy-rate variants of the solver's derandomization idea.","title":"Uniform Distribution of Sequences","url":"https://store.doverpublications.com/products/9780486149998","venue_year":"Wiley, 1974; Dover reprint"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The proof could fail through a quantifier slip in the Borel-Cantelli diagonalization, a non-summable failure probability, or strict-crossing boundary cases at c=1 and a_t=c_t. These look fixable but should be audited line by line.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","strict-crossing audit"],"summary":"Turn the solver's monotone-threshold derandomization into a precise lemma: for the equal-revenue law mu on [e^{-1},1], prove that there is a deterministic sequence a_t such that uniformly over every n and every nonincreasing c_1 >= ... >= c_n, sum_t a_t 1{a_t<c_t} >= sum_t g(c_t) - e_n with e_n=o(n), preferably e_n=O(n^{2/3} sqrt(log n)). Check the grid count, one-sided Hoeffding bound, downward rounding, and Borel-Cantelli construction of one infinite realization.","title":"Audit The Monotone Derandomization Lemma","why_promising":"This directly validates or breaks the solver attempt. If true, it removes randomization from the known-path 1/e upper bound with minimal new machinery."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Standard empirical-process theorems usually assume iid observations, while here the time coordinates are fixed and the threshold sequence is triangular. The monotone-boundary class may need a custom bracketing argument rather than direct citation.","suggested_lenses":["empirical process","entropy bounds","rate sharpening"],"summary":"Reformulate the derandomization lemma as a uniform law of large numbers for the class f_c(t,a)=a 1{a<c_t}, where c_t is nonincreasing in t. Try to prove the needed uniform convergence via bracketing entropy for monotone functions or bounded-variation classes, and compare the resulting rate to the solver's finite-grid union bound.","title":"Empirical-Process Reformulation","why_promising":"A reusable entropy proof would be cleaner than an ad hoc grid argument and may also support infinite-menu or nonstationary variants later."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Ordinary one-dimensional low discrepancy may only control fixed thresholds on prefixes, while the hard class allows many time-varying monotone thresholds. An explicit van der Corput or Kronecker sequence may fail unless interval-threshold discrepancy is controlled.","suggested_lenses":["constructive derandomization","discrepancy theory","counterexample search"],"summary":"Look for a constructive replacement for the probabilistic-method sequence, for example a transformed low-discrepancy sequence a_t=exp(u_t-1), a greedy conditional-expectation construction over finite grid events, or an online discrepancy method. If explicit sequences fail, produce a monotone-threshold counterexample showing which discrepancy notion is actually needed.","title":"Constructive Deterministic Sequence","why_promising":"The current candidate upper bound is nonconstructive. An explicit policy would make the theorem algorithmic and clarify whether standard low-discrepancy tools are strong enough."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The statement can easily mix incompatible quantifier orders: process-aware versus process-unaware policies, deterministic versus randomized policies, pathwise versus expected regret, and exogenous deterministic versus stochastic prices. The lower bound must be checked to apply to deterministic policies as a subset of randomized policies.","suggested_lenses":["theorem synthesis","quantifier audit","lower-upper matching"],"summary":"Combine the derandomized upper bound with CL-027 to state the exact deterministic known-path theorem: there exists one deterministic horizon-oblivious process-unaware policy with limsup_T R_T(P)/T <= 1/e for every deterministic exogenous path P, while there exists a deterministic path P* on which every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e. Then add the exogenous stochastic extension by conditioning on realized paths if the pathwise proof supports it.","title":"Exact Deterministic 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"This would upgrade CL-029 from a randomized expected-regret theorem to a sharp deterministic-policy theorem, resolving the main open item named after iteration 9."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best local distributions may depend on the whole future path and may use left-limit atoms near thresholds. The uniform derandomization becomes nonidentically distributed, and infinite suffix menus still need approximation.","suggested_lenses":["upper-bound framework","infinite-menu approximation","path-dependent characterization"],"summary":"Extend the solver's derandomization method to the path-dependent suffix-menu framework. For a known deterministic path with suffix menus C_t={M_{t,T}:T>t}, choose time-dependent distributions mu_t that achieve the local deficits delta(C_t), then prove a deterministic sequence with R_T <= sum_{t<T} delta(C_t)+o(T) uniformly over horizons. Start with finite C_t, then add an infinite-menu approximation lemma.","title":"Path-Dependent Suffix-Menu Upper Bound","why_promising":"The universal 1/e policy is worst-case sharp but loose on easy paths. This direction could move from a worst-case constant to a genuine known-path anytime characterization."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"This is not necessary for the asymptotic 1/e constant and could become a standalone discrepancy-theory problem. It may consume effort without changing the main theorem.","suggested_lenses":["finite-time rates","discrepancy lower bounds","counterexample search"],"summary":"Study the optimal finite-n error e_n in the monotone-threshold lemma. Prove lower bounds for every deterministic sequence and upper bounds via random construction, empirical-process chaining, or explicit discrepancy sequences. Determine whether the solver's O(n^{2/3} sqrt(log n)) rate is an artifact or near-optimal for this monotone-threshold class.","title":"Finite-Time Discrepancy Of Monotone Thresholds","why_promising":"A rate theorem would strengthen the deterministic upper bound and clarify the exact complexity of serving all horizon-induced monotone suffix-max sequences."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:17:- **Missing proof detail:** The recovery of same-path multi-threshold blocks should explicitly show how the dual’s horizon weights reproduce the CL-026 cancellation of intermediate threshold-round rewards. This is plausible but should not be asserted without the algebra.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:25:- **Singleton terminal block:** \(0^N,c\). The dual should give \(D_{N+1}=0\), matching \(\kappa(\{c\})=0\). This checks that a known single threshold is not falsely hard.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:27:- **Increasing two-threshold block:** \(0^N,1/2,1\). The dual should give \(D_{N+2}=N/4+O(1)\) in the strict implementation and exactly \(N/4\) in left-limit form. This is the minimal regression test.
./iterations/iter_013/consolidation.md:5:N\kappa(C)\le D_{N+m}\le N\kappa(C)+m,
./iterations/iter_012/idea_01/explorer_01.md:116:- Jacob Abernethy, Peter L. Bartlett, Elad Hazan, “Blackwell Approachability and No-Regret Learning are Equivalent,” COLT/PMLR 2011. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v19/abernethy11b.html
./iterations/iter_010/solution_gate.md:5:If the target were only the deterministic-policy derandomization theorem, I see no fatal flaw in the core idea. The monotone-threshold union bound plus Hoeffding plus Borel-Cantelli argument looks repairable. But it is still a proof sketch, not a fully written theorem with exact constants, summable failure probabilities, and final quantifiers.
./iterations/iter_010/solution_gate.md:15:Literature check: I found no direct delayed-market-making derandomization theorem. The relevant sources are background only: Alon-Spencer’s *The Probabilistic Method* for probabilistic-method existence, van der Vaart-Wellner for empirical-process context, and Niederreiter for possible explicit low-discrepancy replacements. These do not affect the gate decision.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/explorer_01.md:69:For increasing same-path blocks \(0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m\), the same dual recovers the accepted \(N\kappa(C)-M\) lower bound; intermediate threshold-round terms are covered by the same cancellation already in CL-026/LB-020.
./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:432:Status: accepted for same-path horizon-oblivious lower bounds. The increasing order of thresholds is essential; otherwise the effective threshold by \(T_j\) is \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), not \(c_j\). Horizon-aware policies, comparator `max`, or repeated-fill rewards invalidate the stated proof.
./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:442:M_i=\sum_{\ell<i}(N_\ell+m_\ell),
./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:572:Proof skeleton. Draw \(A_t\stackrel{iid}{\sim}\mu\). For fixed \(n\), set \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\) and let \(\mathcal D_{n,m}\) be all nonincreasing sequences in the grid \(\{0,1/m,\ldots,1\}\). Then
./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:574:|\mathcal D_{n,m}|\le {n+m\choose m}.
