./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:946:This follows by subtracting the one-level high-threshold cancellation from the two-level dual expression. Status: accepted as a finite-alphabet test-bed identity, not a gluing theorem.
./working_notes/lemma_bank.md:948:Warning: the alternating two-level path need not have exact \(D_n=0\) for arbitrary \(\ell,h\). For example, on \(\ell,h,\ell,h\) with \(0<\ell<h<2\ell\), zero deficit at the high-dominated horizons forces high quotes, creating positive deficit at the intermediate low-dominated horizon. The conflict is bounded in this tiny example, but exact zero should not be used as a blanket sanity check.
./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./working_notes/canonical_summary.md:95:Iteration 10 derandomizes the known-path upper bound in a nonconstructive sense. A probabilistic-method grid/Hoeffding/Borel-Cantelli argument gives a fixed deterministic quote sequence \(a_t\in(e^{-1},1)\) such that, uniformly over all nonincreasing threshold sequences \(c_1\ge\cdots\ge c_n\),
./working_notes/canonical_summary.md:138:and changing only the first \(m\) marginal laws changes every later horizon deficit by at most \(m\). For increasing blocks \(0^N,c_1,\ldots,c_m\), the finite-prefix value satisfies the stress-test bound \(N\kappa(C)\le D_{N+m}\le N\kappa(C)+m\), with exact equality for \(m=2\) in the formal left-limit model. A direct market analogue of the abstract parity obstruction was not found; separated conflicts appear to be diluted by the new quote opportunities that make \(D_n/n\) small. The main open target remains a market-specific localization/gluing theorem, or a genuine market-path separator between \(D_n=o(n)\) and the fixed-envelope criterion.
./working_notes/canonical_summary.md:146:D_{N+L+2}=N\,\frac{c_0(c_1-c_0)}{c_1}.
./iterations/iter_013/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan","relevance":"Useful for treating simultaneous horizon deficits as an approachability-style objective and for checking whether rate-preserving reductions apply to the finite-prefix LP program.","title":"Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 291, 2025"},{"authors":"Shie Mannor, Vianney Perchet, Gilles Stoltz","relevance":"Set-valued payoff ambiguity is a close analogy for horizon-indexed payoff constraints and possible conditional/partial-monitoring formulations.","title":"Set-Valued Approachability and Online Learning with Partial Monitoring","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v15/mannor14a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 15, 2014"},{"authors":"Ehud Lehrer","relevance":"Potentially relevant if the deterministic known-path problem is formulated as approachability with countably many horizon coordinates.","title":"Approachability in Infinite Dimensional Spaces","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s001820200115","venue_year":"International Journal of Game Theory 31(2):253-268, 2002/2003"},{"authors":"Ehud Lehrer","relevance":"Relevant background for one strategy satisfying many comparison criteria over subsets/stages, analogous to one quote sequence serving many horizons.","title":"A Wide Range No-Regret Theorem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0899-8256(03)00032-0","venue_year":"Games and Economic Behavior 42(1):101-115, 2003"},{"authors":"Andrey Bernstein, Nahum Shimkin","relevance":"Response-map approachability may offer a cleaner algorithmic route for vector-deficit feasibility than projection-based approachability.","title":"Response-Based Approachability with Applications to Generalized No-Regret Problems","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v16/bernstein15a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 16:747-773, 2015"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The finite-prefix optimizer may still sacrifice old horizons in a way monotonicity cannot repair; the proof may collapse back to the already accepted fixed-envelope compactness criterion.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","finite LP duality","compactness"],"summary":"Try to prove a market-specific gluing theorem: for deterministic exogenous known paths, D_n(P)=o(n) plus monotonicity of each suffix maximum M_{t,T} in T implies existence of one horizon-oblivious marginal sequence with o(T) regret. Focus on a scale-stability lemma showing finite-prefix solutions can be modified so all dyadic older horizons keep sublinear deficits.","title":"Monotone Gluing From D_n","why_promising":"This is the current sharp open target identified by the solver, and the abstract parity obstruction is not obviously realizable by monotone market suffix maxima."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Market monotonicity may prevent the parity pattern entirely, or any attempted construction may make D_n linear and therefore fail to separate the criteria.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","LP computation","block construction"],"summary":"Attempt a deterministic market path with D_n=o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope, mimicking the solver's abstract parity obstruction. Use rapidly growing blocks whose finite-prefix optimizer alternates between incompatible quote distributions, then compute D_n and the fixed-envelope LP explicitly.","title":"Market Parity Obstruction","why_promising":"A successful example would settle that D_n=o(n) is not sufficient; a failed, well-analyzed attempt would reveal what extra monotone structure a gluing proof can exploit."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The calculations may only confirm known easy behavior and not produce a theorem; stochastic versions introduce conditioning and measurability issues.","suggested_lenses":["stress-test computation","counterexample audit"],"summary":"Compute D_n(P), or tight upper/lower asymptotics, for the existing tail-bad but learnable examples: sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, superexponential uniform spikes, coded-delay variants, and alternating paths. For each, produce explicit marginals and dual certificates.","title":"D_n On Tail Counterexamples","why_promising":"This tests whether D_n is the right decision-relevant replacement for beta/gamma/Delta tails and builds a regression suite for future characterization claims."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The exact correction may depend on threshold-round ordering and strict-crossing conventions; the current O(m) slack may be unavoidable for arbitrary menus.","suggested_lenses":["explicit LP solution","proof strategy"],"summary":"Sharpen the solver's finite increasing-block estimate. For P=0^N,c_1,...,c_m, prove whether D_{N+m}=N kappa(C) exactly in the left-limit model, or identify the exact additive correction from the threshold-round actions. Start with m=3 and compare primal and dual LPs.","title":"Exact Increasing-Block D_n","why_promising":"It would make D_n concrete on the main lower-bound gadget and may expose the local algebra needed for global gluing or counterexamples."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The stage payoffs may not decompose cleanly because V_T^* is horizon-level; infinite-dimensional closedness and norm choices may obscure rather than clarify the problem.","suggested_lenses":["formulation","literature mapping","approachability"],"summary":"Reformulate deterministic known-path anytime design as vector approachability of the negative orthant, with one coordinate per horizon deficit. Investigate whether infinite-dimensional, set-valued, or response-based approachability results give either a gluing theorem or a computable sufficient condition.","title":"Approachability Formulation","why_promising":"The problem is intrinsically one action sequence serving many horizon objectives, which is exactly the kind of simultaneous constraint geometry approachability was built to express."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting modulus may be tautological or no easier to verify than the fixed-envelope criterion; weighted duals may be technically messy.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","LP duality","proof strategy"],"summary":"Define scale-sensitive finite-prefix moduli between D_n=o(n) and the fixed-envelope criterion, such as weighted LPs controlling deficit_T/e_T for all T<=n or dyadic-window values controlling all T in [alpha n,n]. Prove exact compactness equivalences and derive dual certificates.","title":"Scale-Sensitive Prefix Moduli","why_promising":"If D_n is too weak, this may yield a still-checkable characterization that avoids arbitrary envelope feasibility while blocking the solver's old-horizon sacrifice issue."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Stable-comparator conditions may be only sufficient and far from necessary; switching quote levels can be subtle because old quotes remain active and single-fill censoring matters.","suggested_lenses":["sufficient conditions","example analysis"],"summary":"Develop readable sufficient conditions based on the exact comparator V_T^*=sup_b b |{t<T:M_{t,T}>b}|. Examples: one quote b is o(T)-optimal for all large T, the optimal quote lies in a finite menu with few costly switches, or argmax drift is slow in a quantified value metric.","title":"Stable Comparator Conditions","why_promising":"It explains easy paths like alternating 1/2,1 and could provide useful structure below the full LP characterization."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Law-aware horizon-specific pseudo-regret collapses if quantifiers are not fixed; conditional LPs require careful measurable selection and adaptation bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["stochastic formulation","rare-event counterexample","measurability"],"summary":"Extend the deterministic finite-prefix LP to exogenous stochastic processes by using conditional payoff functions phi_{t,T}(a)=E[a 1{max_{t<s<=T}P_s>a}|F_t] and probability-weighted horizon deficits. Test rare-history constructions where essential sup tails are large but expected regret is small.","title":"Conditional Stochastic D_n","why_promising":"The original model allows stochastic prices; a conditional, probability-weighted modulus could connect deterministic path insights with expected-regret learnability while avoiding essential-sup false positives."},{"id":"idea_09","risks":"This improves sufficiency rather than necessity; delayed-bandit results may not map cleanly to public-price full-information counterfactual rewards.","suggested_lenses":["algorithmic sufficiency","literature-to-model map"],"summary":"Translate adaptive delayed-learning ideas based on skipped feedback, outstanding observations, or tracking capacity to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for regret bounds using payoff-weighted skipped mass or observed outstanding-fill complexity rather than a fixed known truncation H.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Algorithms","why_promising":"The literature consistently points away from raw elapsed-delay tails and toward missing-information quantities; this can strengthen the accepted if-direction under unknown tails."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s core mathematical claim is worth pursuing: the monotone-threshold derandomization lemma appears sound after a careful audit. I do not see a counterexample to the grid + Hoeffding + union bound + Borel-Cantelli route.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:11:- **Plausible but incomplete**: the Borel-Cantelli step needs to be written with explicit events and constants. For grid size \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\) and deviation
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:25:- **Plausible but incomplete**: finite exceptional \(n\) from Borel-Cantelli must be absorbed into the claimed \(O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\) error, e.g. by enlarging the realization-dependent constant and using \(\log(n+2)\).
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:52:Hoeffding’s original inequality is the most directly relevant external source. Gao-Wellner and van der Vaart-Wellner are overkill unless the proof is recast as empirical-process bracketing.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:70:- Aad W. van der Vaart and Jon A. Wellner. *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes*, Springer, 1996. https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2. Background for uniform convergence; not needed for the elementary proof.
./iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:72:- Fuchang Gao and Jon A. Wellner. “Entropy Estimate For High Dimensional Monotonic Functions.” arXiv:math/0512641, 2005; *Journal of Multivariate Analysis* 98(9):1751-1764, 2007. https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512641. Relevant only if replacing the elementary grid count with monotone bracketing entropy.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:23:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:41:- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:42:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:68:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:71:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:85:- Dann, Mansour, Mohri, Schneider, and Sivan (ALT/PMLR 2023), "Pseudonorm Approachability and Applications to Regret Minimization," is verified. It develops \(\ell_\infty\)/pseudonorm approachability tools for many regret constraints. Useful if horizon-indexed deficits are recast as approachability constraints; not direct proof support.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:101:- Alon and Spencer, *The Probabilistic Method* (Wiley, 4th ed. 2016), is verified as general background for proving existence by a random construction, union bounds, and Borel-Cantelli. It is not a delayed-market-making source.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:102:- Van der Vaart and Wellner, *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes* (Springer, 1996), is verified and provides empirical-process/bracketing background. It is useful if the monotone-threshold lemma is later written via bracketing rather than the elementary grid proof.
./working_notes/literature_map.md:103:- Gao and Wellner (JMVA 2007/arXiv:math/0512641) is verified. It gives monotone-function entropy context and cautions about high-dimensional monotone classes; it is not needed for the accepted \(O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\) grid proof.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:37:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari. "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 30(1), 2016. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v30i1.10320. Relevance: full-information delayed-feedback/OCO reduction and adaptive-gradient algorithms; possible support for cleaner grid sufficiency variants.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:59:- Jacob Abernethy, Peter L. Bartlett, Elad Hazan. "Blackwell Approachability and No-Regret Learning are Equivalent." COLT 2011, PMLR 19:27-46. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v19/abernethy11b.html. Relevance: possible framework for simultaneous constraint feasibility or prefix-compatibility formulations.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:89:- Shan Xue, Ye Du, Liang Xu. "Adaptive Market Making with Inventory Constraints via Online Learning." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 39(20):21859-21867, 2025. https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i20.35492. Relevance: adjacent online-learning market-making model with inventory constraints; not a delayed-fill tail characterization.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:93:- David Blackwell. "An analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 6(1):1-8, 1956. https://msp.org/pjm/1956/6-1/p01.xhtml. Relevance: classical origin of approachability/vector-payoff feasibility; conceptual background for prefix-compatibility formulations.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:97:- Joon Kwon, Vianney Perchet. "Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability with Partial Monitoring: Optimal Convergence Rates." AISTATS 2017, PMLR 54:604-613. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v54/kwon17a.html. Relevance: approachability with partial monitoring and multi-criteria objectives; possible framework for dynamic prefix-compatibility variants.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:103:- Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli. "Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book." arXiv:2605.19584, 2026; listed on the COLT 2026 accepted-papers page. https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584. Relevance: adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful contrast, not a delayed-fill tail characterization.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:107:- Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang. "Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction." COLT 2026 / arXiv:2602.17577. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577. Relevance: simultaneous approachability with coupled actions; analogy for prefix-compatibility and simultaneous-horizon feasibility.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:115:- Daniele Condorelli, Balázs Szentes. "Information Design in the Holdup Problem." Journal of Political Economy 128(2):681-709, 2020. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1086/704574. Relevance: arbitrary-distribution monopoly-pricing model with related unit-elastic/equal-revenue structure.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:151:- Ioannis Anagnostides, Gabriele Farina, Maxwell Fishelson, Haipeng Luo, Jon Schneider. "Swap Regret Minimization Through Response-Based Approachability." arXiv:2602.06264, 2026. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06264. Relevance: weak approachability/simultaneous-regret analogy if the known-path problem is later formulated as vector-payoff feasibility; not proof support for the \(1/e\) market-making upper bound.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:157:- Noga Alon, Joel H. Spencer. *The Probabilistic Method*, 4th ed. Wiley, 2016. Wiley listing: https://www.wiley.com/en-gb/The%2BProbabilistic%2BMethod%2C%2B4th%2BEdition-p-9781119061953. Relevance: standard background for nonconstructive existence via random constructions, concentration, union bounds, and Borel-Cantelli.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:159:- Aad W. van der Vaart, Jon A. Wellner. *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes: With Applications to Statistics*. Springer Series in Statistics, 1996. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2. Relevance: empirical-process and bracketing background for possible sharper proofs of the monotone-threshold uniform convergence lemma.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:161:- Fuchang Gao, Jon A. Wellner. "Entropy Estimate for High-Dimensional Monotonic Functions." *Journal of Multivariate Analysis* 98(9):1751-1764, 2007. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmva.2006.09.003; arXiv: https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0512641. Relevance: monotone-function entropy context; useful if replacing the elementary grid proof by a bracketing argument.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:183:- Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan. "Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability." Proceedings of the 38th Conference on Learning Theory, PMLR 291:1380-1414, 2025. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html. Relevance: approachability/regret reduction background for possible vector-deficit formulations; not direct proof support for \(D_n\)-gluing.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:199:- Janos Flesch, Rida Laraki, Vianney Perchet. "Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability in Quitting Games." Proceedings of the 29th Conference on Learning Theory, PMLR 49:941-942, 2016. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/flesch16.html. Relevance: weak background for fixed-horizon versus anytime approachability in quitting-game settings; not direct proof support.
./working_notes/bibliography.md:205:- Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balubramanian Sivan. "Pseudonorm Approachability and Applications to Regret Minimization." Proceedings of the 34th International Conference on Algorithmic Learning Theory, PMLR 201:471-509, 2023. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v201/dann23a.html. Relevance: \(\ell_\infty\)/pseudonorm approachability tools for many simultaneous regret constraints; possible formulation background for horizon-indexed deficits, not proof support for \(D_n\)-gluing.
./iterations/iter_003/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Emmanuel Esposito, Saeed Masoudian, Hao Qiu, Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Intermediate observations are a close analogue of public price history that may predict delayed fills; useful for formalizing when predictable tails cease to be harmful.","title":"Delayed Bandits: When Do Intermediate Observations Help?","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/esposito23a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Ciara Pike-Burke, Shipra Agrawal, Csaba Szepesvari, Steffen Grunewalder","relevance":"Now verified from PMLR; relevant background for delayed/aggregated feedback and for distinguishing identifiable full-information fills from harder anonymous feedback.","title":"Bandits with Delayed, Aggregated Anonymous Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/pike-burke18a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2018"},{"authors":"Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich","relevance":"Older full-information delayed prediction reference already listed as unchecked; DBLP/DOI metadata verify bibliographic details and support the delayed-experts background section.","title":"On delayed prediction of individual sequences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136","venue_year":"IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 2002"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"Very recent reduction-style delayed OCO paper with total-delay and outstanding-observation terms; may inform adaptive truncation/oracle inequalities beyond fixed H.","title":"A Reduction from Delayed to Immediate Feedback for Online Convex Optimization with Improved Guarantees","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jiahao Zhang, Tao Lin, Weiqiang Zheng, Zhe Feng, Yifeng Teng, Xiaotie Deng","relevance":"Threshold learning with censored feedback and one-sided Lipschitz assumptions; potentially useful for the continuous quote geometry and limits of discretization-free methods.","title":"Learning Thresholds with Latent Values and Censored Feedback","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=qaKRfobbTg","venue_year":"ICLR 2024"},{"authors":"Jianyu Xu, Yining Wang, Xi Chen, Yu-Xiang Wang","relevance":"Recent dynamic pricing paper with adversarial censoring; adjacent terminology and techniques for censored observations, though not a direct delayed-fill characterization.","title":"Dynamic Pricing with Adversarially-Censored Demands","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06168","venue_year":"arXiv 2025, revised 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The comparator over the whole prefix is a single fixed quote, so cross-block rewards and previous pending quotes can invalidate a naive per-block regret decomposition. Horizon-aware learner families may require an explicit diagonalization argument.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound formalization","randomized learner conditioning"],"summary":"Turn CL-016 into a precise theorem. Fix a universal process-unaware learner, choose dominant deterministic blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1} and M_i=o(N_i), condition on the fixed prefix and the first N_i zeros, use the finite two-continuation lemma to choose c_i causing at least N_i/4 current-block regret, then prove earlier blocks and pending rewards cost only O(M_i). State separately for anytime learners and horizon-indexed learner families.","title":"Prove the concatenated universal block lower bound","why_promising":"This is the closest current route to a genuine necessity-style statement: it converts the accepted finite indistinguishability gadget into a class-uniform impossibility without reviving the false instance-wise claim."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The direction may collapse too: known-law anytime learners might be able to schedule near-optimal quotes by doubling or by Blackwell-style calibration, making no tail condition necessary even without horizon knowledge. Conversely, examples may depend on artificial noncomputability or rapidly changing horizon optima.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","counterexample search","online-to-batch reduction"],"summary":"Investigate the regime between oracle collapse and universal learning: the learner knows the exogenous process law but not the horizon, and must use one anytime policy with pseudo-regret o(T) for every prefix. Either prove a generic anytime collapse via grids/doubling over horizon-optimal fixed quotes, or construct a known process whose horizon-optimal quote switches across dominant horizons so that no single quote sequence tracks all prefixes.","title":"Horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability","why_promising":"The notes identify horizon awareness as the fatal reason instance-wise necessity is vacuous. Removing only that assumption is a clean, separable quantifier change that may yield the weakest nontrivial necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A proposed modulus can become tautological if it merely restates minimax regret. It must produce either an upper-bound algorithm or a lower-bound lemma with checkable hypotheses on histories, continuation values, and payoff scales.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","Le Cam/Yao lower bounds","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail or value-of-information quantity for a process class: after a history, measure pairs of continuations that remain observationally identical until after H but have separated optimal fixed quotes or separated comparator values. Prove a two-continuation lower bound in terms of this separation and compare it with sufficient quantities Γ_T(H), Δ_T(H), and skipped payoff mass.","title":"Replace raw tails by an information obstruction","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that long tails alone are harmless when low-payoff, sparse, or predictable. A continuation-separation modulus directly targets the missing ingredient: unpredictability that affects the optimal quote."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Pathwise regret is a different problem, so it cannot be presented as a fix to the original pseudo-regret theorem without explicit restatement. Sparse vanishing-payoff examples may still refute unweighted β-necessity, requiring a weighted or value-based tail instead.","suggested_lenses":["regret notion comparison","stochastic mixture lower bound","counterexample transfer"],"summary":"Analyze the alternative regret E[sup_a sum_t r_t(a) - sum_t r_t(a_t)]. Revisit the random two-continuation block under this pathwise comparator; unlike pseudo-regret, the hidden terminal state should remain hard. Then test whether β, γ, or an information-weighted tail becomes necessary under this stronger hindsight comparator.","title":"Pathwise comparator variant","why_promising":"The oracle collapse and stochastic-mixture warning are specific to sup_a E[reward]. If the original motivation wanted hindsight over realized prices, this variant may restore a meaningful tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Combining many truncation horizons can introduce extra logarithmic factors and feedback alignment problems; rewards for different H correspond to different surrogate games. Existing bandit/OCO skipping results may not transfer directly despite public prices.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","oracle inequality","literature mapping"],"summary":"Build an adaptive finite-grid algorithm that does not require a known H or known β decay. Target an oracle bound like inf_H {sqrt(T(H+1) log K)+T/K+Γ_T(H)} plus polylog factors, using multi-scale truncation, sleeping experts over H, or skip-set reductions. Map Joulani et al. 2013/2016, Gyorgy-Joulani 2021, Flaspohler et al. 2021, and Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2026 to the public-price full-information setting.","title":"Adaptive truncation and skipping oracle bounds","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted sufficiency theorem from a hand-tuned H(T) proof to a practical anytime theorem and may identify the right algorithmic tail penalty."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The expected contribution must stay small for all horizons while the essential supremum remains large on positive-probability histories. It is easy to accidentally create linear comparator value or a process-aware optimal quote that does not actually achieve small regret.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon calculations","essential-sup stress test"],"summary":"Finish CE-006 by constructing a rare-branch process: with tiny probability p_n reveal a code leading to a long positive-payoff delay L_n, with p_n L_n summable or sublinear over horizons, while conditional on the rare code the tail probability is one for arbitrarily large h. Compute β, γ, Δ_T, Γ_T, and pseudo-regret for a simple learner.","title":"Rare-history essential-sup counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate uniform conditional tail assumptions from ex-ante learnability even at positive payoff scale, clarifying whether γ fails because of predictability, rarity, or both."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The reward functions a 1{threshold>a} have discontinuities, so one-sided Lipschitz tools may require distributional smoothness absent from the adversarial/public-price model. The standard T/K downward grid bound may already be minimax without extra assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","discretization lower bounds","smoothness assumptions"],"summary":"Explore whether threshold/censored-feedback geometry can improve or qualify the grid discretization step. Study f_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H=max_{s≤H} P_{t+s}, and ask whether adaptive partitions, one-sided Lipschitz assumptions, or censored-threshold results yield better continuum regret than T/K, or prove an adversarial lower bound showing the grid loss is unavoidable.","title":"Continuous quote geometry beyond uniform grids","why_promising":"This is independent of tail necessity and could sharpen the sufficiency theorem. The newly checked threshold/censored papers give terminology and possible assumptions for a non-adversarial refinement."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
