./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3529:./logs/iter_007_ideas.stderr.log:2549:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3530:./logs/iter_009_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3536:./logs/iter_008_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Bobby Kleinberg, Renato Paes Leme, Jon Schneider, Yifeng Teng","relevance":"Useful analogy for the prefix-compatibility program: U-calibration is a maximal regret metric over all downstream bounded scoring rules, similar in spirit to one action sequence serving many candidate horizons or continuations.","title":"U-Calibration: Forecasting for an Unknown Agent","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/kleinberg23a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 195, 2023"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Yifan Wu","relevance":"Decision-theoretic calibration loss is a probability-weighted worst-case payoff-loss notion; it may supply terminology and LP analogies for replacing raw delay tails by decision-relevant information loss.","title":"Calibration Error for Decision Making","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13503","venue_year":"FOCS 2024 / arXiv:2404.13503"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan","relevance":"Gives minimax results for simultaneous regret over loss families; relevant as an analogy for simultaneous-prefix and vector-deficit formulations, not as direct support for delayed market making.","title":"Optimal Multiclass U-Calibration Error and Beyond","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=7aFRgCC8Q7","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Konstantina Bairaktari, Lunjia Hu, Huy L. Nguyen, Jonathan Ullman","relevance":"Recent actionable calibration work emphasizes measures that directly upper-bound downstream swap regret; useful terminology for an actionable delay-tail or information-loss statistic.","title":"Testable and Actionable Calibration for Full Swap Regret","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17749","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The cancellation can fail if the policy model leaks the evaluation horizon, if endpoint timing is misstated, or if strict-crossing limits are handled with a fixed eta that scales with N or m.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing cleanup","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Ask an explorer to turn the iteration-8 same-path multi-threshold argument into a formal finite lemma. For path old-prefix length M, then 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, verify for each horizon T_j=M+N+j that the comparator supremum using a quote approaching c_j from below is at least (N+j-1)c_j, while learner rewards from intermediate threshold-round actions before T_j are at most (j-1)c_j. This should yield R_Tj >= N c_j - sum_s A_s 1{A_s<c_j} - M.","title":"Audit The No-Loss Same-Path Lift","why_promising":"If correct, this promotes CL-026 in a stronger form with no O(m) finite-block loss and is the cleanest immediate extension of the accepted two-threshold anytime obstruction."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The proof must track the denominator T_j=M_i+N_i+j and require m_i=o(N_i). It also must be explicit that the learner is process-aware but horizon-oblivious; a horizon-aware oracle avoids the lower bound.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","asymptotic bookkeeping","model specification"],"summary":"Use the finite same-path lemma to build one deterministic known path with blocks 0^{N_i},C_i, where C_i is the m_i-point equal-revenue threshold menu, m_i -> infinity, and M_i+m_i=o(N_i). Prove that every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e.","title":"Diagonal Same-Path 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"It would give the exact same-path analogue of the accepted universal terminal-block 1/e lift and sharply separates anytime learning from horizon-aware oracle policies."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A local randomized primal strategy may not compose into a global anytime upper bound. Strict crossing also requires quotes just below thresholds or a limiting argument.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","tightness check","constructive upper bound"],"summary":"Solve the primal local zero-block game for finite C: minimize over action laws mu the maximum over c in C of c - E_mu[a 1{a<c}]. For equal-revenue thresholds, verify the solver's claim that the uniform law over quotes just below c_i has constant deficit r^m, matching the dual kappa(C). Then characterize the primal optimizer for arbitrary C.","title":"Primal Kappa Geometry","why_promising":"This would certify tightness of the finite multi-threshold lower bound and may reveal whether the block lower bounds are also local upper bounds for known-path anytime design."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The condition may become tautological if defined as the final regret itself. Overlapping future maxima across blocks may prevent additive decomposition into local kappas.","suggested_lenses":["characterization attempt","counterexample search","offline optimization"],"summary":"Formulate the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix problem: choose one action sequence so that V_T^* - reward_T is small for all T. Test candidate compatibility quantities on 0^N,c_1,...,c_m blocks, sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, and superexponential uniform spikes.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Compatibility","why_promising":"This is the most plausible quantifier regime for a real characterization: oracle collapse no longer applies, but raw beta failure is still not necessary."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A lower-bound modulus may not imply sufficiency. Rare events, suffix compensation, and process-aware coding can make any unweighted or purely local condition misleading.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","stress testing","literature mapping"],"summary":"Develop a probability-weighted information-loss modulus based on common-prefix events: p_u times a local kappa_I(U), minus an outside-surplus cap B_u. Compare it with U-calibration and Calibration Decision Loss as decision-relevant replacements for raw error metrics. Test it against CE-001, CE-003, CE-005, and same-path blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It directly targets the main obstruction in the notes: long delayed mass matters only when it is payoff-relevant, unpredictable, frequent enough, and not compensated later."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The all-horizons expected-regret calculation may be delicate. If the hard branch is not revealed early enough, even a process-aware learner may suffer p_i N_i regret; if it is too revealed, the essential-sup hardness may become artificial but still useful as a stress test.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Construct a rare-branch process where, with probability p_i, a code reveals entry into a long equal-revenue multi-threshold block with kappa close to 1/e, but p_i N_i is chosen to be negligible relative to relevant horizons. Compute beta, gamma, conditional kappa, Delta_T, and expected regret for law-aware policies.","title":"Rare-Branch Multi-Threshold Stress Test","why_promising":"This would sharpen CE-006 and show that essential-sup or conditional hardness must be probability-weighted before it can support a necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Most arbitrary-delay results are bandit or OCO rather than public-price full-information market making. The explorer must explicitly map reward-vector observability and skipped payoff loss.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","model mapping","literature audit"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping or outstanding-observation delayed-learning algorithms to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for a bound of the form delayed-learning term plus payoff-weighted skipped-fill loss plus T/K, without knowing a tail function beta or gamma in advance.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It strengthens the settled sufficiency side and aligns the theorem with modern delayed-feedback literature, where missing information and skipped observations are often more relevant than raw elapsed delay."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3537:./logs/iter_006_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2046:iterations/iter_006/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli","relevance":"Adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful terminology and contrast for delayed-fill feedback.","title":"Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"New delayed OCO model with tracking capacity and delayed-weighted reductions; relevant to adaptive truncation/skipping and outstanding-information bounds.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang","relevance":"Develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions, a possible framework for prefix-compatibility or simultaneous-horizon guarantees.","title":"Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider","relevance":"Recent simultaneous-regret work; useful analogy for one sequence of predictions/actions satisfying many downstream losses or horizons.","title":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jason D. Hartline","relevance":"Contains equal-revenue distribution and monopoly-pricing/revenue-curve background that may directly match the multi-threshold block LP.","title":"Mechanism Design and Approximation, Chapter 4: Bayesian Approximation","url":"https://jasonhartline.com/MDnA/MDnA-ch4.pdf","venue_year":"online textbook chapter"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The 1/e constant may fail under finite-support approximation, strict crossing, or transfer to concatenated paths; multi-horizon same-path versions must control intermediate quote rewards when the number of thresholds grows.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","lower-bound constants","mechanism-design analogy","strict-threshold cleanup"],"summary":"Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).","title":"Multi-Threshold Block Constant","why_promising":"This is a concrete, separately checkable extension of the accepted 1/4 lemma and may strengthen the lower-bound constant while connecting kappa to known posted-price geometry."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The prefix-local comparator v_u must be a valid lower bound on the global fixed-action comparator; same-prefix laws can fail for stochastic candidates; rare events and suffix compensation can otherwise create false positives.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's kappa transfer into a precise lemma. Work on finite grids and finite candidate set U first: condition on a common-prefix event E, assume the learner's prefix action marginals are identical for all u in U, define prefix payoffs phi_{u,t}, and include an explicit outside-surplus cap B_u plus probability weight Pr(E). Prove max_u regret_u >= |I| kappa_I(U)-B, or identify the missing hypotheses.","title":"Kappa Transfer Lemma","why_promising":"It would convert the current prefix-compatibility heuristic into an accepted reusable lower-bound tool, and every assumption can be criticized directly."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A full characterization may become tautological or computational rather than structural; the best fixed comparator couples all times through one quote, while the learner can vary quotes by time.","suggested_lenses":["offline characterization","finite examples","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Study the process-aware but horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix design problem. Given a known path P_1,P_2,..., characterize when there exists one action sequence a_t whose rewards track V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) for all T. Use paths 0^N,c_0,c_1 and multi-threshold paths as test cases, and look for a horizon-compatibility modulus that gives both lower and upper bounds.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Characterization","why_promising":"This targets the one quantifier regime where oracle collapse does not apply and where the same-path obstruction is genuinely instance-wise."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Delayed OCO/bandit results may not map cleanly because market-making feedback is full-information only after public threshold crossings and horizon censoring creates missing zero rewards; tracking all K grid arms may be too expensive without exploiting nesting.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature map","model translation"],"summary":"Replace fixed H truncation with adaptive skipping or tracking-capacity algorithms on the grid problem. Define skipped payoff mass or outstanding weighted feedback, use public prices to close all grid arms below P_s, and attempt a bound in terms of skipped value, total outstanding observations, or capacity C rather than beta(H). Compare with Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2025/2026 and delayed-weighted FTRL reductions.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It could produce a stronger and more usable sufficiency theorem that does not require knowing a tail schedule H_T and aligns with current delayed-learning literature."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"If the rare branch is fully coded, the example may only refute essential-sup tail conditions for process-aware learners; if it is not coded, regret may become nontrivial. All-horizon expected-value bounds need careful summability choices.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","conditional tails","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Finish a rare-history construction where positive-probability branches contain arbitrarily long payoff-relevant delays, keeping beta or gamma large through essential sup, while their ex-ante contribution is negligible and/or the branch code makes the optimal quote known. Choose p_n, L_n, and amplitudes so expected regret is o(T) but conditional tails never decay.","title":"Rare-History Tail Failure","why_promising":"This directly tests whether essential-sup tail assumptions are too strong even after payoff weighting, and would sharpen CE-006 into a reusable obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Always quoting 1/2 must be checked for every inter-spike horizon, not just spike endpoints; older blocks and strict-crossing nonattainment can add small but annoying terms.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample polishing","horizon accounting","Gamma stress test"],"summary":"Formalize the superexponential uniform-spike process: P_{S_n}=U_n with U_n uniform on [0,1], zeros otherwise, S_{n+1}/S_n -> infinity. Prove an anytime simple policy, likely always 1/2, has o(T) regret for all T, while gamma stays bounded away from zero and Delta_{S_n}(H)=Omega(S_n) for H=o(S_n). Determine whether Gamma_T(H) is also linear along spike horizons.","title":"Uniform-Spike Counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate predictable delayed reward mass from learning difficulty more cleanly than the beta-only sparse-vanishing-spike example."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Approachability controls vector averages under observed vector payoffs, while the market-making deficits involve future-censored rewards and possibly unknown candidate horizons; feedback availability may block a direct algorithm.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithmic upper bound","finite candidate reduction"],"summary":"Try to turn prefix compatibility from a lower-bound modulus into an algorithmic sufficient condition. For a finite set of candidate horizons or continuations, define vector deficits against each candidate and ask whether simultaneous Blackwell approachability gives one action rule with small regret to all candidates when kappa-like feasibility gaps vanish or are small.","title":"Simultaneous-Approachability Upper Bound","why_promising":"It offers a possible positive counterpart to the prefix-incompatibility lower bounds and uses new simultaneous approachability literature rather than only delayed-feedback tools."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may drift away from the stated pseudo-regret problem; raw beta may still be the wrong statistic even under pathwise regret, so the output may be only a taxonomy.","suggested_lenses":["regret-notion comparison","counterexample search","quantifier taxonomy"],"summary":"Systematically compare pseudo-regret sup_a E[reward(a)] with pathwise regret E[sup_a reward(a)-learner] and with endogenous/adaptive prices. Re-test oracle collapse, stochastic two-continuation mixtures, sparse spikes, and coded delays under each notion to identify whether any tail necessity statement can be salvaged.","title":"Alternative Regret Notions","why_promising":"The original iff may be false mainly because of the pseudo-regret and horizon/law-aware quantifiers; this direction could clarify exactly which nearby formulations remain mathematically meaningful."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3553:./logs/iter_007_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2415:iterations/iter_007/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3554:./logs/iter_007_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Zhi Chen, Zhenyu Hu, Ruiqin Wang","relevance":"Develops a minimax pricing/robust screening duality with equal-revenue worst-case distributions, closely adjacent to the local κ dual and useful for checking whether the finite-support calculation is known.","title":"Screening with Limited Information: A Dual Perspective","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2022.0016","venue_year":"Operations Research 72(4), 2024"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann, Benjamin Brooks, Stephen Morris","relevance":"Foundational price-discrimination/information-design source for surplus-frontier and buyer-optimal segmentation geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint.","title":"The Limits of Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20130848","venue_year":"American Economic Review, 2015"},{"authors":"Rachel Cummings, Nikhil R. Devanur, Zhiyi Huang, Xiangning Wang","relevance":"Algorithmic LP treatment of finite-support price discrimination and buyer/seller surplus objectives; may contain reusable finite-dimensional duality tools for the discrete κ program.","title":"Algorithmic Price Discrimination","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975994.149","venue_year":"SODA, 2020"},{"authors":"Shao-Heng Ko, Kamesh Munagala","relevance":"Uses finite discrete equal-revenue signals in price-discrimination constructions; useful adjacent evidence and possible proof technique for finite-support equal-revenue threshold laws.","title":"Optimal Price Discrimination for Randomized Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.04687","venue_year":"EC 2022; ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation, 2024"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The theorem may need small corrections for normalization, zero mass, or strict crossing; the solver's proof might prove only a slightly different support-count convention if support at 0 is mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing edge cases","LP duality"],"summary":"Turn the solver's finite-m result into a polished proposition: for any nonnegative threshold law with at most m positive support points, E[Z] - sup_a a Pr(Z>a) <= ((m-1)/m)^m, with equality for the discrete equal-revenue support. Check every reduction step: removing zero atoms, scaling top support to 1, raising values below rho to rho without increasing rho, preserving support size, using left-limit revenue under strict crossing, and proving tightness from the proposed tail probabilities.","title":"Certify The Finite-m Equal-Revenue Theorem","why_promising":"This is the main new progress in the solver attempt; if accepted, it upgrades CL-023 and supplies a reusable exact constant for all multi-threshold lower-bound lifts."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The local dual certificate only lower-bounds regret when the common-prefix action law is genuinely shared and outside rewards are capped; otherwise this repeats the failed local-κ-as-standalone mistake.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower-bound transfer","quantifier check"],"summary":"Use the finite-m theorem to prove a finite multi-continuation block lemma. For a zero block of length N and terminal continuation c in a chosen m-threshold set C_m, show that every learner with the same zero-block action law across continuations suffers at least N κ(C_m) regret on one continuation, where κ(C_m)=((m-1)/m)^m for the equal-revenue set. Then lift it to the universal/process-unaware dominant-block construction with the old-reward cap M_i.","title":"Finite-m Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly converts the local finite-m geometry into a stronger version of the accepted 1/4 universal block obstruction."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The limit constant may require choosing m_i and N_i on a delicate diagonal schedule, and the final theorem must not be overstated as instance-wise necessity.","suggested_lenses":["asymptotic construction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Diagonalize the finite-m block lower bound with m_i -> infinity and N_i dominating both previous length M_i and any threshold-menu overhead. The target theorem is a universal/process-unaware deterministic block class with limsup R_T/T >= 1/e - o(1), improving the binary 1/4 constant. Verify whether the same construction keeps beta and gamma tails large in the intended sense.","title":"Approach A 1/e Universal Lower Bound","why_promising":"The solver's constant tends to 1/e, matching the accepted continuum κ cap, so this may identify the sharp local minimax obstruction for universal delayed market making."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Intermediate threshold-round quotes may create extra learner rewards or comparator terms; an O(m) loss is probably safe, but removing it may require careful sup-comparator bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["same-path horizon-oblivious proof","strict-crossing bookkeeping"],"summary":"Prove the same-path multi-threshold lift on deterministic known blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, with regret evaluated after each threshold time. Show that a horizon-oblivious policy's shared zero-block actions imply max_j R_{T_j} >= N κ(C)-M-O(m), and then specialize to equal-revenue C_m. Track exactly how quotes posted at intermediate threshold times affect both learner reward and comparator value.","title":"Multi-Threshold Anytime Prefix Conflict","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted known-path horizon-oblivious obstruction from the binary 1/4 case to the finite-m and possibly 1/e regime."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A local mixed strategy need not compose into a global no-regret algorithm because actions affect many future horizons and censoring changes the payoff matrix over time.","suggested_lenses":["minimax primal","algorithm design","finite computations"],"summary":"Derive the primal optimizer for the finite-m zero-block game: find an action distribution μ minimizing sup_c [c - E_{a~μ} a 1{a<c}] for the equal-revenue threshold set, and take the m -> infinity limit. Use small finite LP computations to guess a closed form. Then ask whether this local defensive distribution can be used as a building block for upper bounds in simultaneous-prefix or universal settings.","title":"Find The Primal Equal-Revenue Action Law","why_promising":"The dual constant is valuable, but an explicit primal strategy would clarify sharpness and may point toward matching upper bounds or approachability formulations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting condition may become tautological if it just restates regret; it needs either an algorithmic consequence or a clean lower-bound transfer theorem.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","simultaneous-prefix characterization"],"summary":"Recast the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path problem as simultaneous approximation of prefix value functions V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a). Define vector deficits over a moving family of horizons and test whether Blackwell/approachability-style conditions characterize when one action sequence can keep all prefix regrets sublinear. Use sparse vanishing spikes as an easy positive test and multi-threshold blocks as negative tests.","title":"Known-Path Simultaneous-Prefix Framework","why_promising":"It targets the quantifier regime where oracle collapse fails but raw beta-necessity is still false, so it may be the right replacement for tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction must avoid being trivialized by horizon-aware oracle policies; it should be stated for expected regret under initial law and with explicit learner information.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","rare-event weighting"],"summary":"Build a rare-prefix process where, conditional on a rare event, a long multi-threshold block has κ close to 1/e, but the event probability p_i makes p_i N_i=o(T). Show that essential-sup or conditional κ tails remain large while expected regret is small for a simple process-aware policy. This would formalize the rare-history obstruction with the sharper finite-m gadget.","title":"Rare Multi-Threshold Prefix Stress Test","why_promising":"It would clarify why any information-tail necessity must be frequency-weighted, not based on essential sup or unweighted local hardness."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The exact finite-m constant may not appear in the literature under the same normalization, so the search can return only adjacent terminology rather than a citation.","suggested_lenses":["literature audit","terminology mapping"],"summary":"Search the price-discrimination, robust pricing, and buyer-optimal signaling literature for the exact finite-support problem: maximize E[Z]-sup_a a Pr(Z>a) over m-point laws on [0,1]. Compare the solver proof with discrete equal-revenue distributions, finite-signal buyer-optimal schemes, and minimax pricing duals; report whether the result is known, a corollary, or apparently new.","title":"Check Whether CL-023 Is Known","why_promising":"A citation or known formulation would strengthen the final writeup and prevent duplicating an existing finite-support monopoly-pricing theorem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3555:./logs/iter_006_ideas.stderr.log:2193:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli","relevance":"Adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful terminology and contrast for delayed-fill feedback.","title":"Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"New delayed OCO model with tracking capacity and delayed-weighted reductions; relevant to adaptive truncation/skipping and outstanding-information bounds.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang","relevance":"Develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions, a possible framework for prefix-compatibility or simultaneous-horizon guarantees.","title":"Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider","relevance":"Recent simultaneous-regret work; useful analogy for one sequence of predictions/actions satisfying many downstream losses or horizons.","title":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jason D. Hartline","relevance":"Contains equal-revenue distribution and monopoly-pricing/revenue-curve background that may directly match the multi-threshold block LP.","title":"Mechanism Design and Approximation, Chapter 4: Bayesian Approximation","url":"https://jasonhartline.com/MDnA/MDnA-ch4.pdf","venue_year":"online textbook chapter"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The 1/e constant may fail under finite-support approximation, strict crossing, or transfer to concatenated paths; multi-horizon same-path versions must control intermediate quote rewards when the number of thresholds grows.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","lower-bound constants","mechanism-design analogy","strict-threshold cleanup"],"summary":"Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).","title":"Multi-Threshold Block Constant","why_promising":"This is a concrete, separately checkable extension of the accepted 1/4 lemma and may strengthen the lower-bound constant while connecting kappa to known posted-price geometry."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The prefix-local comparator v_u must be a valid lower bound on the global fixed-action comparator; same-prefix laws can fail for stochastic candidates; rare events and suffix compensation can otherwise create false positives.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's kappa transfer into a precise lemma. Work on finite grids and finite candidate set U first: condition on a common-prefix event E, assume the learner's prefix action marginals are identical for all u in U, define prefix payoffs phi_{u,t}, and include an explicit outside-surplus cap B_u plus probability weight Pr(E). Prove max_u regret_u >= |I| kappa_I(U)-B, or identify the missing hypotheses.","title":"Kappa Transfer Lemma","why_promising":"It would convert the current prefix-compatibility heuristic into an accepted reusable lower-bound tool, and every assumption can be criticized directly."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A full characterization may become tautological or computational rather than structural; the best fixed comparator couples all times through one quote, while the learner can vary quotes by time.","suggested_lenses":["offline characterization","finite examples","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Study the process-aware but horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix design problem. Given a known path P_1,P_2,..., characterize when there exists one action sequence a_t whose rewards track V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) for all T. Use paths 0^N,c_0,c_1 and multi-threshold paths as test cases, and look for a horizon-compatibility modulus that gives both lower and upper bounds.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Characterization","why_promising":"This targets the one quantifier regime where oracle collapse does not apply and where the same-path obstruction is genuinely instance-wise."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Delayed OCO/bandit results may not map cleanly because market-making feedback is full-information only after public threshold crossings and horizon censoring creates missing zero rewards; tracking all K grid arms may be too expensive without exploiting nesting.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature map","model translation"],"summary":"Replace fixed H truncation with adaptive skipping or tracking-capacity algorithms on the grid problem. Define skipped payoff mass or outstanding weighted feedback, use public prices to close all grid arms below P_s, and attempt a bound in terms of skipped value, total outstanding observations, or capacity C rather than beta(H). Compare with Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2025/2026 and delayed-weighted FTRL reductions.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It could produce a stronger and more usable sufficiency theorem that does not require knowing a tail schedule H_T and aligns with current delayed-learning literature."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"If the rare branch is fully coded, the example may only refute essential-sup tail conditions for process-aware learners; if it is not coded, regret may become nontrivial. All-horizon expected-value bounds need careful summability choices.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","conditional tails","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Finish a rare-history construction where positive-probability branches contain arbitrarily long payoff-relevant delays, keeping beta or gamma large through essential sup, while their ex-ante contribution is negligible and/or the branch code makes the optimal quote known. Choose p_n, L_n, and amplitudes so expected regret is o(T) but conditional tails never decay.","title":"Rare-History Tail Failure","why_promising":"This directly tests whether essential-sup tail assumptions are too strong even after payoff weighting, and would sharpen CE-006 into a reusable obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Always quoting 1/2 must be checked for every inter-spike horizon, not just spike endpoints; older blocks and strict-crossing nonattainment can add small but annoying terms.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample polishing","horizon accounting","Gamma stress test"],"summary":"Formalize the superexponential uniform-spike process: P_{S_n}=U_n with U_n uniform on [0,1], zeros otherwise, S_{n+1}/S_n -> infinity. Prove an anytime simple policy, likely always 1/2, has o(T) regret for all T, while gamma stays bounded away from zero and Delta_{S_n}(H)=Omega(S_n) for H=o(S_n). Determine whether Gamma_T(H) is also linear along spike horizons.","title":"Uniform-Spike Counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate predictable delayed reward mass from learning difficulty more cleanly than the beta-only sparse-vanishing-spike example."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Approachability controls vector averages under observed vector payoffs, while the market-making deficits involve future-censored rewards and possibly unknown candidate horizons; feedback availability may block a direct algorithm.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithmic upper bound","finite candidate reduction"],"summary":"Try to turn prefix compatibility from a lower-bound modulus into an algorithmic sufficient condition. For a finite set of candidate horizons or continuations, define vector deficits against each candidate and ask whether simultaneous Blackwell approachability gives one action rule with small regret to all candidates when kappa-like feasibility gaps vanish or are small.","title":"Simultaneous-Approachability Upper Bound","why_promising":"It offers a possible positive counterpart to the prefix-incompatibility lower bounds and uses new simultaneous approachability literature rather than only delayed-feedback tools."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may drift away from the stated pseudo-regret problem; raw beta may still be the wrong statistic even under pathwise regret, so the output may be only a taxonomy.","suggested_lenses":["regret-notion comparison","counterexample search","quantifier taxonomy"],"summary":"Systematically compare pseudo-regret sup_a E[reward(a)] with pathwise regret E[sup_a reward(a)-learner] and with endogenous/adaptive prices. Re-test oracle collapse, stochastic two-continuation mixtures, sparse spikes, and coded delays under each notion to identify whether any tail necessity statement can be salvaged.","title":"Alternative Regret Notions","why_promising":"The original iff may be false mainly because of the pseudo-regret and horizon/law-aware quantifiers; this direction could clarify exactly which nearby formulations remain mathematically meaningful."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3640:./logs/iter_008_synthesis.stderr.log:2725:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Bobby Kleinberg, Renato Paes Leme, Jon Schneider, Yifeng Teng","relevance":"Useful analogy for the prefix-compatibility program: U-calibration is a maximal regret metric over all downstream bounded scoring rules, similar in spirit to one action sequence serving many candidate horizons or continuations.","title":"U-Calibration: Forecasting for an Unknown Agent","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/kleinberg23a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 195, 2023"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Yifan Wu","relevance":"Decision-theoretic calibration loss is a probability-weighted worst-case payoff-loss notion; it may supply terminology and LP analogies for replacing raw delay tails by decision-relevant information loss.","title":"Calibration Error for Decision Making","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13503","venue_year":"FOCS 2024 / arXiv:2404.13503"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan","relevance":"Gives minimax results for simultaneous regret over loss families; relevant as an analogy for simultaneous-prefix and vector-deficit formulations, not as direct support for delayed market making.","title":"Optimal Multiclass U-Calibration Error and Beyond","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=7aFRgCC8Q7","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Konstantina Bairaktari, Lunjia Hu, Huy L. Nguyen, Jonathan Ullman","relevance":"Recent actionable calibration work emphasizes measures that directly upper-bound downstream swap regret; useful terminology for an actionable delay-tail or information-loss statistic.","title":"Testable and Actionable Calibration for Full Swap Regret","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17749","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The cancellation can fail if the policy model leaks the evaluation horizon, if endpoint timing is misstated, or if strict-crossing limits are handled with a fixed eta that scales with N or m.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing cleanup","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Ask an explorer to turn the iteration-8 same-path multi-threshold argument into a formal finite lemma. For path old-prefix length M, then 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, verify for each horizon T_j=M+N+j that the comparator supremum using a quote approaching c_j from below is at least (N+j-1)c_j, while learner rewards from intermediate threshold-round actions before T_j are at most (j-1)c_j. This should yield R_Tj >= N c_j - sum_s A_s 1{A_s<c_j} - M.","title":"Audit The No-Loss Same-Path Lift","why_promising":"If correct, this promotes CL-026 in a stronger form with no O(m) finite-block loss and is the cleanest immediate extension of the accepted two-threshold anytime obstruction."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The proof must track the denominator T_j=M_i+N_i+j and require m_i=o(N_i). It also must be explicit that the learner is process-aware but horizon-oblivious; a horizon-aware oracle avoids the lower bound.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","asymptotic bookkeeping","model specification"],"summary":"Use the finite same-path lemma to build one deterministic known path with blocks 0^{N_i},C_i, where C_i is the m_i-point equal-revenue threshold menu, m_i -> infinity, and M_i+m_i=o(N_i). Prove that every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e.","title":"Diagonal Same-Path 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"It would give the exact same-path analogue of the accepted universal terminal-block 1/e lift and sharply separates anytime learning from horizon-aware oracle policies."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A local randomized primal strategy may not compose into a global anytime upper bound. Strict crossing also requires quotes just below thresholds or a limiting argument.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","tightness check","constructive upper bound"],"summary":"Solve the primal local zero-block game for finite C: minimize over action laws mu the maximum over c in C of c - E_mu[a 1{a<c}]. For equal-revenue thresholds, verify the solver's claim that the uniform law over quotes just below c_i has constant deficit r^m, matching the dual kappa(C). Then characterize the primal optimizer for arbitrary C.","title":"Primal Kappa Geometry","why_promising":"This would certify tightness of the finite multi-threshold lower bound and may reveal whether the block lower bounds are also local upper bounds for known-path anytime design."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The condition may become tautological if defined as the final regret itself. Overlapping future maxima across blocks may prevent additive decomposition into local kappas.","suggested_lenses":["characterization attempt","counterexample search","offline optimization"],"summary":"Formulate the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix problem: choose one action sequence so that V_T^* - reward_T is small for all T. Test candidate compatibility quantities on 0^N,c_1,...,c_m blocks, sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, and superexponential uniform spikes.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Compatibility","why_promising":"This is the most plausible quantifier regime for a real characterization: oracle collapse no longer applies, but raw beta failure is still not necessary."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A lower-bound modulus may not imply sufficiency. Rare events, suffix compensation, and process-aware coding can make any unweighted or purely local condition misleading.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","stress testing","literature mapping"],"summary":"Develop a probability-weighted information-loss modulus based on common-prefix events: p_u times a local kappa_I(U), minus an outside-surplus cap B_u. Compare it with U-calibration and Calibration Decision Loss as decision-relevant replacements for raw error metrics. Test it against CE-001, CE-003, CE-005, and same-path blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It directly targets the main obstruction in the notes: long delayed mass matters only when it is payoff-relevant, unpredictable, frequent enough, and not compensated later."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The all-horizons expected-regret calculation may be delicate. If the hard branch is not revealed early enough, even a process-aware learner may suffer p_i N_i regret; if it is too revealed, the essential-sup hardness may become artificial but still useful as a stress test.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Construct a rare-branch process where, with probability p_i, a code reveals entry into a long equal-revenue multi-threshold block with kappa close to 1/e, but p_i N_i is chosen to be negligible relative to relevant horizons. Compute beta, gamma, conditional kappa, Delta_T, and expected regret for law-aware policies.","title":"Rare-Branch Multi-Threshold Stress Test","why_promising":"This would sharpen CE-006 and show that essential-sup or conditional hardness must be probability-weighted before it can support a necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Most arbitrary-delay results are bandit or OCO rather than public-price full-information market making. The explorer must explicitly map reward-vector observability and skipped payoff loss.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","model mapping","literature audit"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping or outstanding-observation delayed-learning algorithms to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for a bound of the form delayed-learning term plus payoff-weighted skipped-fill loss plus T/K, without knowing a tail function beta or gamma in advance.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It strengthens the settled sufficiency side and aligns the theorem with modern delayed-feedback literature, where missing information and skipped observations are often more relevant than raw elapsed delay."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
