./logs/iter_013_solver.stdout.log:31:The lower bound is the same cancellation used in the same-path multi-threshold lower bound: at horizon \(T_j=N+j\), the comparator value is \((N+j-1)c_j\), while threshold-round learner payoff before \(T_j\) is at most \((j-1)c_j\), leaving the zero-block deficit. The upper bound uses the local primal law for \(C\) on the zero block and pays at most \(m\) for crude threshold-round slack.
./logs/iter_013_solver.stdout.log:35:D_{N+2}=N\,\frac{c_1(c_2-c_1)}{c_2}
./logs/iter_013_solver.stdout.log:40:For \(0^N,c\), \(D_{N+1}=0\). One formal quote \(c^-\) serves the only nontrivial horizon.
./logs/iter_013_solver.stdout.log:70:- Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan. “Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability.” COLT 2025, PMLR 291:1380-1414. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html. Relevant for treating simultaneous-horizon deficits as approachability-style vector objectives; not a direct market-making theorem.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:679:Iteration 10 derandomizes the known-path upper bound in a nonconstructive sense. A probabilistic-method grid/Hoeffding/Borel-Cantelli argument gives a fixed deterministic quote sequence \(a_t\in(e^{-1},1)\) such that, uniformly over all nonincreasing threshold sequences \(c_1\ge\cdots\ge c_n\),
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1149:M_i=\sum_{\ell<i}(N_\ell+m_\ell),
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1226:Proof: sample \(a_t\) iid from the equal-revenue law \(d\mu(a)=a^{-1}1\{e^{-1}\le a\le1\}\,da\), union-bound Hoeffding deviations over nonincreasing \(m_n\)-grid threshold sequences with \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\), round arbitrary thresholds downward, and use Borel-Cantelli to freeze one good realization.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1734:Proof skeleton. Draw \(A_t\stackrel{iid}{\sim}\mu\). For fixed \(n\), set \(m_n=\lceil n^{1/3}\rceil\) and let \(\mathcal D_{n,m}\) be all nonincreasing sequences in the grid \(\{0,1/m,\ldots,1\}\). Then
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1736:|\mathcal D_{n,m}|\le {n+m\choose m}.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1738:For fixed \(d\in\mathcal D_{n,m}\), the variables
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1750:u_n=\sqrt{\frac n2\left(\log|\mathcal D_{n,m_n}|+4\log(n+2)\right)}.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:1752:A union bound gives failure probability at most \((n+2)^{-4}\), hence summable. By Borel-Cantelli, with positive probability all but finitely many grid events hold. Freeze one realization satisfying them and absorb the finitely many exceptional \(n\)'s into \(C\).
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2132:Status: accepted for same-path horizon-oblivious lower bounds. The increasing order of thresholds is essential; otherwise the effective threshold by \(T_j\) is \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), not \(c_j\). Horizon-aware policies, comparator `max`, or repeated-fill rewards invalidate the stated proof.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2142:M_i=\sum_{\ell<i}(N_\ell+m_\ell),
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2299:Invalid as stated. The identity that a zero-block quote \(A_s\) has traded by \(T_j\) iff \(A_s<c_j\) uses \(0<c_1<\cdots<c_m\) in path order. If the thresholds are unsorted, the effective threshold by \(T_j\) is \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), and the \(\kappa(C)\) formula for the displayed order no longer follows directly.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2488:at horizon \(T_2=N+2\), a zero-block action \(A_s=3/4\) has already traded at the first threshold \(1\). But \(A_s<c_2\) is false when \(c_2=1/2\). Thus the zero-block learner payoff by \(T_j\) is governed by \(\max_{\ell\le j}c_\ell\), not by the displayed \(c_j\), unless the path order is increasing.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2575:A successful example would settle that D_n=o(n) is not sufficient; a failed, well-analyzed attempt would reveal what extra monotone structure a gluing proof can exploit.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2669:- Flesch, Laraki, Perchet, “Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability in Quitting Games,” COLT/PMLR 2016: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/flesch16.html
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2670:- Dann, Mansour, Mohri, Schneider, Sivan, “Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability,” COLT/PMLR 2025: https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2675:- Janos Flesch, Rida Laraki, Vianney Perchet. “Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability in Quitting Games.” COLT 2016, PMLR 49:941-942. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/flesch16.html. Relevant because it explicitly separates fixed-horizon and anytime approachability conditions.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2677:- Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan. “Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability.” COLT 2025, PMLR 291:1380-1414. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html. Relevant background for treating simultaneous horizon deficits as approachability-style vector objectives.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2708:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2726:- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2727:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2753:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2756:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2772:- Alon and Spencer, *The Probabilistic Method* (Wiley, 4th ed. 2016), is verified as general background for proving existence by a random construction, union bounds, and Borel-Cantelli. It is not a delayed-market-making source.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2773:- Van der Vaart and Wellner, *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes* (Springer, 1996), is verified and provides empirical-process/bracketing background. It is useful if the monotone-threshold lemma is later written via bracketing rather than the elementary grid proof.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2774:- Gao and Wellner (JMVA 2007/arXiv:math/0512641) is verified. It gives monotone-function entropy context and cautions about high-dimensional monotone classes; it is not needed for the accepted \(O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\) grid proof.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2835:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari. "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 30(1), 2016. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v30i1.10320. Relevance: full-information delayed-feedback/OCO reduction and adaptive-gradient algorithms; possible support for cleaner grid sufficiency variants.
