./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:4137:iterations/iter_013/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan","relevance":"Useful for treating simultaneous horizon deficits as an approachability-style objective and for checking whether rate-preserving reductions apply to the finite-prefix LP program.","title":"Rate-Preserving Reductions for Blackwell Approachability","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v291/dann25a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 291, 2025"},{"authors":"Shie Mannor, Vianney Perchet, Gilles Stoltz","relevance":"Set-valued payoff ambiguity is a close analogy for horizon-indexed payoff constraints and possible conditional/partial-monitoring formulations.","title":"Set-Valued Approachability and Online Learning with Partial Monitoring","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v15/mannor14a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 15, 2014"},{"authors":"Ehud Lehrer","relevance":"Potentially relevant if the deterministic known-path problem is formulated as approachability with countably many horizon coordinates.","title":"Approachability in Infinite Dimensional Spaces","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s001820200115","venue_year":"International Journal of Game Theory 31(2):253-268, 2002/2003"},{"authors":"Ehud Lehrer","relevance":"Relevant background for one strategy satisfying many comparison criteria over subsets/stages, analogous to one quote sequence serving many horizons.","title":"A Wide Range No-Regret Theorem","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0899-8256(03)00032-0","venue_year":"Games and Economic Behavior 42(1):101-115, 2003"},{"authors":"Andrey Bernstein, Nahum Shimkin","relevance":"Response-map approachability may offer a cleaner algorithmic route for vector-deficit feasibility than projection-based approachability.","title":"Response-Based Approachability with Applications to Generalized No-Regret Problems","url":"https://jmlr.org/papers/v16/bernstein15a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 16:747-773, 2015"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The finite-prefix optimizer may still sacrifice old horizons in a way monotonicity cannot repair; the proof may collapse back to the already accepted fixed-envelope compactness criterion.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","finite LP duality","compactness"],"summary":"Try to prove a market-specific gluing theorem: for deterministic exogenous known paths, D_n(P)=o(n) plus monotonicity of each suffix maximum M_{t,T} in T implies existence of one horizon-oblivious marginal sequence with o(T) regret. Focus on a scale-stability lemma showing finite-prefix solutions can be modified so all dyadic older horizons keep sublinear deficits.","title":"Monotone Gluing From D_n","why_promising":"This is the current sharp open target identified by the solver, and the abstract parity obstruction is not obviously realizable by monotone market suffix maxima."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Market monotonicity may prevent the parity pattern entirely, or any attempted construction may make D_n linear and therefore fail to separate the criteria.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","LP computation","block construction"],"summary":"Attempt a deterministic market path with D_n=o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope, mimicking the solver's abstract parity obstruction. Use rapidly growing blocks whose finite-prefix optimizer alternates between incompatible quote distributions, then compute D_n and the fixed-envelope LP explicitly.","title":"Market Parity Obstruction","why_promising":"A successful example would settle that D_n=o(n) is not sufficient; a failed, well-analyzed attempt would reveal what extra monotone structure a gluing proof can exploit."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The calculations may only confirm known easy behavior and not produce a theorem; stochastic versions introduce conditioning and measurability issues.","suggested_lenses":["stress-test computation","counterexample audit"],"summary":"Compute D_n(P), or tight upper/lower asymptotics, for the existing tail-bad but learnable examples: sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, superexponential uniform spikes, coded-delay variants, and alternating paths. For each, produce explicit marginals and dual certificates.","title":"D_n On Tail Counterexamples","why_promising":"This tests whether D_n is the right decision-relevant replacement for beta/gamma/Delta tails and builds a regression suite for future characterization claims."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The exact correction may depend on threshold-round ordering and strict-crossing conventions; the current O(m) slack may be unavoidable for arbitrary menus.","suggested_lenses":["explicit LP solution","proof strategy"],"summary":"Sharpen the solver's finite increasing-block estimate. For P=0^N,c_1,...,c_m, prove whether D_{N+m}=N kappa(C) exactly in the left-limit model, or identify the exact additive correction from the threshold-round actions. Start with m=3 and compare primal and dual LPs.","title":"Exact Increasing-Block D_n","why_promising":"It would make D_n concrete on the main lower-bound gadget and may expose the local algebra needed for global gluing or counterexamples."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The stage payoffs may not decompose cleanly because V_T^* is horizon-level; infinite-dimensional closedness and norm choices may obscure rather than clarify the problem.","suggested_lenses":["formulation","literature mapping","approachability"],"summary":"Reformulate deterministic known-path anytime design as vector approachability of the negative orthant, with one coordinate per horizon deficit. Investigate whether infinite-dimensional, set-valued, or response-based approachability results give either a gluing theorem or a computable sufficient condition.","title":"Approachability Formulation","why_promising":"The problem is intrinsically one action sequence serving many horizon objectives, which is exactly the kind of simultaneous constraint geometry approachability was built to express."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting modulus may be tautological or no easier to verify than the fixed-envelope criterion; weighted duals may be technically messy.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","LP duality","proof strategy"],"summary":"Define scale-sensitive finite-prefix moduli between D_n=o(n) and the fixed-envelope criterion, such as weighted LPs controlling deficit_T/e_T for all T<=n or dyadic-window values controlling all T in [alpha n,n]. Prove exact compactness equivalences and derive dual certificates.","title":"Scale-Sensitive Prefix Moduli","why_promising":"If D_n is too weak, this may yield a still-checkable characterization that avoids arbitrary envelope feasibility while blocking the solver's old-horizon sacrifice issue."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Stable-comparator conditions may be only sufficient and far from necessary; switching quote levels can be subtle because old quotes remain active and single-fill censoring matters.","suggested_lenses":["sufficient conditions","example analysis"],"summary":"Develop readable sufficient conditions based on the exact comparator V_T^*=sup_b b |{t<T:M_{t,T}>b}|. Examples: one quote b is o(T)-optimal for all large T, the optimal quote lies in a finite menu with few costly switches, or argmax drift is slow in a quantified value metric.","title":"Stable Comparator Conditions","why_promising":"It explains easy paths like alternating 1/2,1 and could provide useful structure below the full LP characterization."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Law-aware horizon-specific pseudo-regret collapses if quantifiers are not fixed; conditional LPs require careful measurable selection and adaptation bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["stochastic formulation","rare-event counterexample","measurability"],"summary":"Extend the deterministic finite-prefix LP to exogenous stochastic processes by using conditional payoff functions phi_{t,T}(a)=E[a 1{max_{t<s<=T}P_s>a}|F_t] and probability-weighted horizon deficits. Test rare-history constructions where essential sup tails are large but expected regret is small.","title":"Conditional Stochastic D_n","why_promising":"The original model allows stochastic prices; a conditional, probability-weighted modulus could connect deterministic path insights with expected-regret learnability while avoiding essential-sup false positives."},{"id":"idea_09","risks":"This improves sufficiency rather than necessity; delayed-bandit results may not map cleanly to public-price full-information counterfactual rewards.","suggested_lenses":["algorithmic sufficiency","literature-to-model map"],"summary":"Translate adaptive delayed-learning ideas based on skipped feedback, outstanding observations, or tracking capacity to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for regret bounds using payoff-weighted skipped mass or observed outstanding-fill complexity rather than a fixed known truncation H.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Algorithms","why_promising":"The literature consistently points away from raw elapsed-delay tails and toward missing-information quantities; this can strengthen the accepted if-direction under unknown tails."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:4234:The lower bound is the same cancellation used in the same-path multi-threshold lower bound: at horizon \(T_j=N+j\), the comparator value is \((N+j-1)c_j\), while threshold-round learner payoff before \(T_j\) is at most \((j-1)c_j\), leaving the zero-block deficit. The upper bound uses the local primal law for \(C\) on the zero block and pays at most \(m\) for crude threshold-round slack.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:4238:D_{N+2}=N\,\frac{c_1(c_2-c_1)}{c_2}
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:4243:For \(0^N,c\), \(D_{N+1}=0\). One formal quote \(c^-\) serves the only nontrivial horizon.
