./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2104:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari. "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 30(1), 2016. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v30i1.10320. Relevance: full-information delayed-feedback/OCO reduction and adaptive-gradient algorithms; possible support for cleaner grid sufficiency variants.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2126:- Jacob Abernethy, Peter L. Bartlett, Elad Hazan. "Blackwell Approachability and No-Regret Learning are Equivalent." COLT 2011, PMLR 19:27-46. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v19/abernethy11b.html. Relevance: possible framework for simultaneous constraint feasibility or prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2156:- Shan Xue, Ye Du, Liang Xu. "Adaptive Market Making with Inventory Constraints via Online Learning." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 39(20):21859-21867, 2025. https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i20.35492. Relevance: adjacent online-learning market-making model with inventory constraints; not a delayed-fill tail characterization.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2160:- David Blackwell. "An analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs." Pacific Journal of Mathematics 6(1):1-8, 1956. https://msp.org/pjm/1956/6-1/p01.xhtml. Relevance: classical origin of approachability/vector-payoff feasibility; conceptual background for prefix-compatibility formulations.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2164:- Joon Kwon, Vianney Perchet. "Online Learning and Blackwell Approachability with Partial Monitoring: Optimal Convergence Rates." AISTATS 2017, PMLR 54:604-613. https://proceedings.mlr.press/v54/kwon17a.html. Relevance: approachability with partial monitoring and multi-criteria objectives; possible framework for dynamic prefix-compatibility variants.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2170:- Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli. "Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book." arXiv:2605.19584, 2026; listed on the COLT 2026 accepted-papers page. https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584. Relevance: adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful contrast, not a delayed-fill tail characterization.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2174:- Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang. "Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction." COLT 2026 / arXiv:2602.17577. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577. Relevance: simultaneous approachability with coupled actions; analogy for prefix-compatibility and simultaneous-horizon feasibility.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2182:- Daniele Condorelli, Balázs Szentes. "Information Design in the Holdup Problem." Journal of Political Economy 128(2):681-709, 2020. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1086/704574. Relevance: arbitrary-distribution monopoly-pricing model with related unit-elastic/equal-revenue structure.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2218:- Ioannis Anagnostides, Gabriele Farina, Maxwell Fishelson, Haipeng Luo, Jon Schneider. "Swap Regret Minimization Through Response-Based Approachability." arXiv:2602.06264, 2026. https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06264. Relevance: weak approachability/simultaneous-regret analogy if the known-path problem is later formulated as vector-payoff feasibility; not proof support for the \(1/e\) market-making upper bound.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2242:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2260:- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2261:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2283:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2286:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
