./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6268:./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:3:The main derandomization idea looks mathematically plausible. I did not find a counterexample to the monotone-threshold uniform convergence lemma. The proof can likely be made rigorous by an elementary grid/bracketing plus Hoeffding plus Borel-Cantelli argument.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6272:./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:27:- “Borel-Cantelli yields an infinite deterministic realization” is only sketched. It is probably true, but the proof needs the actual failure probability:
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6278:./iterations/iter_010/solution_critic_01.md:58:- Aad W. van der Vaart and Jon A. Wellner. *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes*, Springer, 1996. https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-2545-2. Relevant as general background for uniform convergence over indexed function classes; the current proof uses only a finite bracketing special case.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6295:./iterations/iter_003/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Emmanuel Esposito, Saeed Masoudian, Hao Qiu, Dirk van der Hoeven, Nicolò Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Intermediate observations are a close analogue of public price history that may predict delayed fills; useful for formalizing when predictable tails cease to be harmful.","title":"Delayed Bandits: When Do Intermediate Observations Help?","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/esposito23a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Ciara Pike-Burke, Shipra Agrawal, Csaba Szepesvari, Steffen Grunewalder","relevance":"Now verified from PMLR; relevant background for delayed/aggregated feedback and for distinguishing identifiable full-information fills from harder anonymous feedback.","title":"Bandits with Delayed, Aggregated Anonymous Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/pike-burke18a.html","venue_year":"ICML/PMLR 2018"},{"authors":"Marcelo J. Weinberger, Erik Ordentlich","relevance":"Older full-information delayed prediction reference already listed as unchecked; DBLP/DOI metadata verify bibliographic details and support the delayed-experts background section.","title":"On delayed prediction of individual sequences","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2002.1013136","venue_year":"IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 2002"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"Very recent reduction-style delayed OCO paper with total-delay and outstanding-observation terms; may inform adaptive truncation/oracle inequalities beyond fixed H.","title":"A Reduction from Delayed to Immediate Feedback for Online Convex Optimization with Improved Guarantees","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02634","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jiahao Zhang, Tao Lin, Weiqiang Zheng, Zhe Feng, Yifeng Teng, Xiaotie Deng","relevance":"Threshold learning with censored feedback and one-sided Lipschitz assumptions; potentially useful for the continuous quote geometry and limits of discretization-free methods.","title":"Learning Thresholds with Latent Values and Censored Feedback","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=qaKRfobbTg","venue_year":"ICLR 2024"},{"authors":"Jianyu Xu, Yining Wang, Xi Chen, Yu-Xiang Wang","relevance":"Recent dynamic pricing paper with adversarial censoring; adjacent terminology and techniques for censored observations, though not a direct delayed-fill characterization.","title":"Dynamic Pricing with Adversarially-Censored Demands","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06168","venue_year":"arXiv 2025, revised 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The comparator over the whole prefix is a single fixed quote, so cross-block rewards and previous pending quotes can invalidate a naive per-block regret decomposition. Horizon-aware learner families may require an explicit diagonalization argument.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound formalization","randomized learner conditioning"],"summary":"Turn CL-016 into a precise theorem. Fix a universal process-unaware learner, choose dominant deterministic blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1} and M_i=o(N_i), condition on the fixed prefix and the first N_i zeros, use the finite two-continuation lemma to choose c_i causing at least N_i/4 current-block regret, then prove earlier blocks and pending rewards cost only O(M_i). State separately for anytime learners and horizon-indexed learner families.","title":"Prove the concatenated universal block lower bound","why_promising":"This is the closest current route to a genuine necessity-style statement: it converts the accepted finite indistinguishability gadget into a class-uniform impossibility without reviving the false instance-wise claim."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The direction may collapse too: known-law anytime learners might be able to schedule near-optimal quotes by doubling or by Blackwell-style calibration, making no tail condition necessary even without horizon knowledge. Conversely, examples may depend on artificial noncomputability or rapidly changing horizon optima.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","counterexample search","online-to-batch reduction"],"summary":"Investigate the regime between oracle collapse and universal learning: the learner knows the exogenous process law but not the horizon, and must use one anytime policy with pseudo-regret o(T) for every prefix. Either prove a generic anytime collapse via grids/doubling over horizon-optimal fixed quotes, or construct a known process whose horizon-optimal quote switches across dominant horizons so that no single quote sequence tracks all prefixes.","title":"Horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability","why_promising":"The notes identify horizon awareness as the fatal reason instance-wise necessity is vacuous. Removing only that assumption is a clean, separable quantifier change that may yield the weakest nontrivial necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A proposed modulus can become tautological if it merely restates minimax regret. It must produce either an upper-bound algorithm or a lower-bound lemma with checkable hypotheses on histories, continuation values, and payoff scales.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","Le Cam/Yao lower bounds","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail or value-of-information quantity for a process class: after a history, measure pairs of continuations that remain observationally identical until after H but have separated optimal fixed quotes or separated comparator values. Prove a two-continuation lower bound in terms of this separation and compare it with sufficient quantities Γ_T(H), Δ_T(H), and skipped payoff mass.","title":"Replace raw tails by an information obstruction","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that long tails alone are harmless when low-payoff, sparse, or predictable. A continuation-separation modulus directly targets the missing ingredient: unpredictability that affects the optimal quote."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Pathwise regret is a different problem, so it cannot be presented as a fix to the original pseudo-regret theorem without explicit restatement. Sparse vanishing-payoff examples may still refute unweighted β-necessity, requiring a weighted or value-based tail instead.","suggested_lenses":["regret notion comparison","stochastic mixture lower bound","counterexample transfer"],"summary":"Analyze the alternative regret E[sup_a sum_t r_t(a) - sum_t r_t(a_t)]. Revisit the random two-continuation block under this pathwise comparator; unlike pseudo-regret, the hidden terminal state should remain hard. Then test whether β, γ, or an information-weighted tail becomes necessary under this stronger hindsight comparator.","title":"Pathwise comparator variant","why_promising":"The oracle collapse and stochastic-mixture warning are specific to sup_a E[reward]. If the original motivation wanted hindsight over realized prices, this variant may restore a meaningful tail characterization."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Combining many truncation horizons can introduce extra logarithmic factors and feedback alignment problems; rewards for different H correspond to different surrogate games. Existing bandit/OCO skipping results may not transfer directly despite public prices.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","oracle inequality","literature mapping"],"summary":"Build an adaptive finite-grid algorithm that does not require a known H or known β decay. Target an oracle bound like inf_H {sqrt(T(H+1) log K)+T/K+Γ_T(H)} plus polylog factors, using multi-scale truncation, sleeping experts over H, or skip-set reductions. Map Joulani et al. 2013/2016, Gyorgy-Joulani 2021, Flaspohler et al. 2021, and Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2026 to the public-price full-information setting.","title":"Adaptive truncation and skipping oracle bounds","why_promising":"This would strengthen the accepted sufficiency theorem from a hand-tuned H(T) proof to a practical anytime theorem and may identify the right algorithmic tail penalty."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The expected contribution must stay small for all horizons while the essential supremum remains large on positive-probability histories. It is easy to accidentally create linear comparator value or a process-aware optimal quote that does not actually achieve small regret.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon calculations","essential-sup stress test"],"summary":"Finish CE-006 by constructing a rare-branch process: with tiny probability p_n reveal a code leading to a long positive-payoff delay L_n, with p_n L_n summable or sublinear over horizons, while conditional on the rare code the tail probability is one for arbitrarily large h. Compute β, γ, Δ_T, Γ_T, and pseudo-regret for a simple learner.","title":"Rare-history essential-sup counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate uniform conditional tail assumptions from ex-ante learnability even at positive payoff scale, clarifying whether γ fails because of predictability, rarity, or both."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The reward functions a 1{threshold>a} have discontinuities, so one-sided Lipschitz tools may require distributional smoothness absent from the adversarial/public-price model. The standard T/K downward grid bound may already be minimax without extra assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","discretization lower bounds","smoothness assumptions"],"summary":"Explore whether threshold/censored-feedback geometry can improve or qualify the grid discretization step. Study f_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H=max_{s≤H} P_{t+s}, and ask whether adaptive partitions, one-sided Lipschitz assumptions, or censored-threshold results yield better continuum regret than T/K, or prove an adversarial lower bound showing the grid loss is unavoidable.","title":"Continuous quote geometry beyond uniform grids","why_promising":"This is independent of tail necessity and could sharpen the sufficiency theorem. The newly checked threshold/censored papers give terminology and possible assumptions for a non-adversarial refinement."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6312:./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:No direct conflict found. Sion’s minimax theorem supports the finite convex minimax step, but finite zero-sum game duality is already enough. Blackwell is useful background for approachability/vector-payoff framing, not direct support for this market-making transfer.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6315:./iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:71:- David Blackwell, “An analog of the minimax theorem for vector payoffs,” *Pacific Journal of Mathematics* 6(1):1-8, 1956. https://projecteuclid.org/journals/pacific-journal-of-mathematics/volume-6/issue-1/An-analog-of-the-minimax-theorem-for-vector-payoffs/pjm/1103044235.short. Relevance: approachability/vector-payoff background only.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6324:./iterations/iter_008/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Bobby Kleinberg, Renato Paes Leme, Jon Schneider, Yifeng Teng","relevance":"Useful analogy for the prefix-compatibility program: U-calibration is a maximal regret metric over all downstream bounded scoring rules, similar in spirit to one action sequence serving many candidate horizons or continuations.","title":"U-Calibration: Forecasting for an Unknown Agent","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/kleinberg23a.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 195, 2023"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Yifan Wu","relevance":"Decision-theoretic calibration loss is a probability-weighted worst-case payoff-loss notion; it may supply terminology and LP analogies for replacing raw delay tails by decision-relevant information loss.","title":"Calibration Error for Decision Making","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13503","venue_year":"FOCS 2024 / arXiv:2404.13503"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Spandan Senapati, Vatsal Sharan","relevance":"Gives minimax results for simultaneous regret over loss families; relevant as an analogy for simultaneous-prefix and vector-deficit formulations, not as direct support for delayed market making.","title":"Optimal Multiclass U-Calibration Error and Beyond","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=7aFRgCC8Q7","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Konstantina Bairaktari, Lunjia Hu, Huy L. Nguyen, Jonathan Ullman","relevance":"Recent actionable calibration work emphasizes measures that directly upper-bound downstream swap regret; useful terminology for an actionable delay-tail or information-loss statistic.","title":"Testable and Actionable Calibration for Full Swap Regret","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17749","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The cancellation can fail if the policy model leaks the evaluation horizon, if endpoint timing is misstated, or if strict-crossing limits are handled with a fixed eta that scales with N or m.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","strict-crossing cleanup","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Ask an explorer to turn the iteration-8 same-path multi-threshold argument into a formal finite lemma. For path old-prefix length M, then 0^N,c_1,...,c_m, verify for each horizon T_j=M+N+j that the comparator supremum using a quote approaching c_j from below is at least (N+j-1)c_j, while learner rewards from intermediate threshold-round actions before T_j are at most (j-1)c_j. This should yield R_Tj >= N c_j - sum_s A_s 1{A_s<c_j} - M.","title":"Audit The No-Loss Same-Path Lift","why_promising":"If correct, this promotes CL-026 in a stronger form with no O(m) finite-block loss and is the cleanest immediate extension of the accepted two-threshold anytime obstruction."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The proof must track the denominator T_j=M_i+N_i+j and require m_i=o(N_i). It also must be explicit that the learner is process-aware but horizon-oblivious; a horizon-aware oracle avoids the lower bound.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","asymptotic bookkeeping","model specification"],"summary":"Use the finite same-path lemma to build one deterministic known path with blocks 0^{N_i},C_i, where C_i is the m_i-point equal-revenue threshold menu, m_i -> infinity, and M_i+m_i=o(N_i). Prove that every horizon-oblivious policy has limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/e.","title":"Diagonal Same-Path 1/e Theorem","why_promising":"It would give the exact same-path analogue of the accepted universal terminal-block 1/e lift and sharply separates anytime learning from horizon-aware oracle policies."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A local randomized primal strategy may not compose into a global anytime upper bound. Strict crossing also requires quotes just below thresholds or a limiting argument.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","tightness check","constructive upper bound"],"summary":"Solve the primal local zero-block game for finite C: minimize over action laws mu the maximum over c in C of c - E_mu[a 1{a<c}]. For equal-revenue thresholds, verify the solver's claim that the uniform law over quotes just below c_i has constant deficit r^m, matching the dual kappa(C). Then characterize the primal optimizer for arbitrary C.","title":"Primal Kappa Geometry","why_promising":"This would certify tightness of the finite multi-threshold lower bound and may reveal whether the block lower bounds are also local upper bounds for known-path anytime design."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The condition may become tautological if defined as the final regret itself. Overlapping future maxima across blocks may prevent additive decomposition into local kappas.","suggested_lenses":["characterization attempt","counterexample search","offline optimization"],"summary":"Formulate the process-aware horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix problem: choose one action sequence so that V_T^* - reward_T is small for all T. Test candidate compatibility quantities on 0^N,c_1,...,c_m blocks, sparse vanishing spikes, power-of-two spikes, and superexponential uniform spikes.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Compatibility","why_promising":"This is the most plausible quantifier regime for a real characterization: oracle collapse no longer applies, but raw beta failure is still not necessary."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A lower-bound modulus may not imply sufficiency. Rare events, suffix compensation, and process-aware coding can make any unweighted or purely local condition misleading.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","stress testing","literature mapping"],"summary":"Develop a probability-weighted information-loss modulus based on common-prefix events: p_u times a local kappa_I(U), minus an outside-surplus cap B_u. Compare it with U-calibration and Calibration Decision Loss as decision-relevant replacements for raw error metrics. Test it against CE-001, CE-003, CE-005, and same-path blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It directly targets the main obstruction in the notes: long delayed mass matters only when it is payoff-relevant, unpredictable, frequent enough, and not compensated later."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The all-horizons expected-regret calculation may be delicate. If the hard branch is not revealed early enough, even a process-aware learner may suffer p_i N_i regret; if it is too revealed, the essential-sup hardness may become artificial but still useful as a stress test.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Construct a rare-branch process where, with probability p_i, a code reveals entry into a long equal-revenue multi-threshold block with kappa close to 1/e, but p_i N_i is chosen to be negligible relative to relevant horizons. Compute beta, gamma, conditional kappa, Delta_T, and expected regret for law-aware policies.","title":"Rare-Branch Multi-Threshold Stress Test","why_promising":"This would sharpen CE-006 and show that essential-sup or conditional hardness must be probability-weighted before it can support a necessity theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Most arbitrary-delay results are bandit or OCO rather than public-price full-information market making. The explorer must explicitly map reward-vector observability and skipped payoff loss.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","model mapping","literature audit"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping or outstanding-observation delayed-learning algorithms to the grid market-making reduction. Aim for a bound of the form delayed-learning term plus payoff-weighted skipped-fill loss plus T/K, without knowing a tail function beta or gamma in advance.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It strengthens the settled sufficiency side and aligns the theorem with modern delayed-feedback literature, where missing information and skipped observations are often more relevant than raw elapsed delay."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6333:./iterations/iter_006/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Davide Maran, Marcello Restelli","relevance":"Adjacent online market-making regret model with action-dependent order-book feedback; useful terminology and contrast for delayed-fill feedback.","title":"Online Market Making and the Value of Observing the Order Book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19584","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Alexander Ryabchenko, Idan Attias, Daniel M. Roy","relevance":"New delayed OCO model with tracking capacity and delayed-weighted reductions; relevant to adaptive truncation/skipping and outstanding-information bounds.","title":"Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.11711","venue_year":"arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Lunjia Hu, Kevin Tian, Chutong Yang","relevance":"Develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions, a possible framework for prefix-compatibility or simultaneous-horizon guarantees.","title":"Simultaneous Blackwell Approachability and Applications to Multiclass Omniprediction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17577","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Rafael Frongillo, Haipeng Luo, Nishant A. Mehta, Jon Schneider","relevance":"Recent simultaneous-regret work; useful analogy for one sequence of predictions/actions satisfying many downstream losses or horizons.","title":"Toward Simultaneously Optimal Regret in U-Calibration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.18527","venue_year":"COLT 2026 / arXiv 2026"},{"authors":"Jason D. Hartline","relevance":"Contains equal-revenue distribution and monopoly-pricing/revenue-curve background that may directly match the multi-threshold block LP.","title":"Mechanism Design and Approximation, Chapter 4: Bayesian Approximation","url":"https://jasonhartline.com/MDnA/MDnA-ch4.pdf","venue_year":"online textbook chapter"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The 1/e constant may fail under finite-support approximation, strict crossing, or transfer to concatenated paths; multi-horizon same-path versions must control intermediate quote rewards when the number of thresholds grows.","suggested_lenses":["LP duality","lower-bound constants","mechanism-design analogy","strict-threshold cleanup"],"summary":"Generalize the two-threshold zero-block lemma to a finite terminal threshold set C. The dual gap should be kappa(C)=sup_lambda E_lambda[C]-sup_a a Pr_lambda(C>a), i.e. welfare minus optimal posted-price revenue. Optimize over lambda and test whether the best zero-block constant is 1/e, achieved approximately by an equal-revenue-type distribution; then lift to universal block concatenations and same-path multi-horizon blocks 0^N,c_1,...,c_m with m=o(N).","title":"Multi-Threshold Block Constant","why_promising":"This is a concrete, separately checkable extension of the accepted 1/4 lemma and may strengthen the lower-bound constant while connecting kappa to known posted-price geometry."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The prefix-local comparator v_u must be a valid lower bound on the global fixed-action comparator; same-prefix laws can fail for stochastic candidates; rare events and suffix compensation can otherwise create false positives.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","formalization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's kappa transfer into a precise lemma. Work on finite grids and finite candidate set U first: condition on a common-prefix event E, assume the learner's prefix action marginals are identical for all u in U, define prefix payoffs phi_{u,t}, and include an explicit outside-surplus cap B_u plus probability weight Pr(E). Prove max_u regret_u >= |I| kappa_I(U)-B, or identify the missing hypotheses.","title":"Kappa Transfer Lemma","why_promising":"It would convert the current prefix-compatibility heuristic into an accepted reusable lower-bound tool, and every assumption can be criticized directly."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A full characterization may become tautological or computational rather than structural; the best fixed comparator couples all times through one quote, while the learner can vary quotes by time.","suggested_lenses":["offline characterization","finite examples","upper-lower matching"],"summary":"Study the process-aware but horizon-oblivious deterministic-path regime as an offline simultaneous-prefix design problem. Given a known path P_1,P_2,..., characterize when there exists one action sequence a_t whose rewards track V_T^*=sup_a sum_{t<=T} r_t(a) for all T. Use paths 0^N,c_0,c_1 and multi-threshold paths as test cases, and look for a horizon-compatibility modulus that gives both lower and upper bounds.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Characterization","why_promising":"This targets the one quantifier regime where oracle collapse does not apply and where the same-path obstruction is genuinely instance-wise."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Delayed OCO/bandit results may not map cleanly because market-making feedback is full-information only after public threshold crossings and horizon censoring creates missing zero rewards; tracking all K grid arms may be too expensive without exploiting nesting.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature map","model translation"],"summary":"Replace fixed H truncation with adaptive skipping or tracking-capacity algorithms on the grid problem. Define skipped payoff mass or outstanding weighted feedback, use public prices to close all grid arms below P_s, and attempt a bound in terms of skipped value, total outstanding observations, or capacity C rather than beta(H). Compare with Ryabchenko-Attias-Roy 2025/2026 and delayed-weighted FTRL reductions.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"It could produce a stronger and more usable sufficiency theorem that does not require knowing a tail schedule H_T and aligns with current delayed-learning literature."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"If the rare branch is fully coded, the example may only refute essential-sup tail conditions for process-aware learners; if it is not coded, regret may become nontrivial. All-horizon expected-value bounds need careful summability choices.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","conditional tails","quantifier audit"],"summary":"Finish a rare-history construction where positive-probability branches contain arbitrarily long payoff-relevant delays, keeping beta or gamma large through essential sup, while their ex-ante contribution is negligible and/or the branch code makes the optimal quote known. Choose p_n, L_n, and amplitudes so expected regret is o(T) but conditional tails never decay.","title":"Rare-History Tail Failure","why_promising":"This directly tests whether essential-sup tail assumptions are too strong even after payoff weighting, and would sharpen CE-006 into a reusable obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Always quoting 1/2 must be checked for every inter-spike horizon, not just spike endpoints; older blocks and strict-crossing nonattainment can add small but annoying terms.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample polishing","horizon accounting","Gamma stress test"],"summary":"Formalize the superexponential uniform-spike process: P_{S_n}=U_n with U_n uniform on [0,1], zeros otherwise, S_{n+1}/S_n -> infinity. Prove an anytime simple policy, likely always 1/2, has o(T) regret for all T, while gamma stays bounded away from zero and Delta_{S_n}(H)=Omega(S_n) for H=o(S_n). Determine whether Gamma_T(H) is also linear along spike horizons.","title":"Uniform-Spike Counterexample","why_promising":"It would separate predictable delayed reward mass from learning difficulty more cleanly than the beta-only sparse-vanishing-spike example."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Approachability controls vector averages under observed vector payoffs, while the market-making deficits involve future-censored rewards and possibly unknown candidate horizons; feedback availability may block a direct algorithm.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithmic upper bound","finite candidate reduction"],"summary":"Try to turn prefix compatibility from a lower-bound modulus into an algorithmic sufficient condition. For a finite set of candidate horizons or continuations, define vector deficits against each candidate and ask whether simultaneous Blackwell approachability gives one action rule with small regret to all candidates when kappa-like feasibility gaps vanish or are small.","title":"Simultaneous-Approachability Upper Bound","why_promising":"It offers a possible positive counterpart to the prefix-incompatibility lower bounds and uses new simultaneous approachability literature rather than only delayed-feedback tools."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This may drift away from the stated pseudo-regret problem; raw beta may still be the wrong statistic even under pathwise regret, so the output may be only a taxonomy.","suggested_lenses":["regret-notion comparison","counterexample search","quantifier taxonomy"],"summary":"Systematically compare pseudo-regret sup_a E[reward(a)] with pathwise regret E[sup_a reward(a)-learner] and with endogenous/adaptive prices. Re-test oracle collapse, stochastic two-continuation mixtures, sparse spikes, and coded delays under each notion to identify whether any tail necessity statement can be salvaged.","title":"Alternative Regret Notions","why_promising":"The original iff may be false mainly because of the pseudo-regret and horizon/law-aware quantifiers; this direction could clarify exactly which nearby formulations remain mathematically meaningful."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
