./logs/iter_008_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1492:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
./logs/iter_008_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1510:- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
./logs/iter_008_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1511:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
