./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2389:- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2407:- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2408:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2430:- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2433:- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2449:- Alon and Spencer, *The Probabilistic Method* (Wiley, 4th ed. 2016), is verified as general background for proving existence by a random construction, union bounds, and Borel-Cantelli. It is not a delayed-market-making source.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2450:- Van der Vaart and Wellner, *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes* (Springer, 1996), is verified and provides empirical-process/bracketing background. It is useful if the monotone-threshold lemma is later written via bracketing rather than the elementary grid proof.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2451:- Gao and Wellner (JMVA 2007/arXiv:math/0512641) is verified. It gives monotone-function entropy context and cautions about high-dimensional monotone classes; it is not needed for the accepted \(O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\) grid proof.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:2508:- Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari. "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms." Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence 30(1), 2016. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v30i1.10320. Relevance: full-information delayed-feedback/OCO reduction and adaptive-gradient algorithms; possible support for cleaner grid sufficiency variants.
